WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:10.990
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: we should mark that because
we certainly wouldn't all be sitting around

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:18.430
the table here, working so much more collaboratively
together for patients across the country without

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:24.160
your contribution over the last 18 months,
which has been really significant. We also

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.740
wouldn't have such a high-performing NHS without
your contribution. Not just in the last 18

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.360
months but over the last three decades. From
starting out in learning disability all the

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.390
way through to the centre of the NHS here.
So on behalf of everyone I'd just like to

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:46.460
thank you for your passion, your professionalism,
right down to the last second in the job,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:51.360
and your deep commitment which I know is very,
very genuine to patients and citizens across

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.300
the country. So, thank you.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: Well, thank you for that. You

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.879
embarrassed me, Dido.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Good.

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:59.579
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: So I shall try and get over
my embarrassment and speak very briefly. I

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:04.040
mean, first, every job I have held in the
NHS since I started from frontline care with

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:09.990
people with learning disabilities has been
a privilege, and this job over the last 18

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:15.770
months has been no different. It is a great
pleasure to me to see NHS England and NHS

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.141
improvement come together. It's entirely the
right thing. We've had an interesting ride,

00:01:20.141 --> 00:01:28.479
I think all of us, senior employees and members
of the team over the last 18 months, and we

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:35.630
obviously enter a new phase now. At a personal
level, it's been a huge privilege and I think

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:41.520
the organisation is now set for real opportunity
with the long-term plan, and we'll talk about

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.740
that, I'm sure, in a minute. So, all I can
say is to thank the board for their kind words

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:52.649
and to wish the new management team, Simon
and Amanda and the rest of you, all the best

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:57.259
in the important and vital work that you continue
to do on behalf of our patients. So thank

00:01:57.259 --> 00:01:59.750
you very much.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: We look forward to watching

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:05.820
all three of you do fantastic things in the
next chapters of your lives. So, thank you.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.550
With that, first item on the agenda now, David
as chair's report, do you want to '

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.660
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, I'll just be very brief.
Maybe illustrate three things, really. Firstly,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:22.819
in visits to Leeds, where the north-east and
actually Valencia in Spain, I've seen how

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:27.819
joined up care really makes a huge difference.
If we can bring together primary care, community

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:35.019
care, and secondary care, then we can deliver
a much better service to our patients.

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:40.640
Secondly, I am on the board of Genomics England,
and I've been out to both Manchester and Newcastle

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.640
in the last sort of months and have seen how
genomic medicine is now beginning to become

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:53.000
mainstream as an offering to our patients,
if you like. And actually seeing someone who

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:59.900
is alive today because some of these new car-T
and gene therapies just brings it home, actually.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.790
That this is a really important new branch
of medicine.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:13.040
And then thirdly, I had around the table discussion
group with about 20 people from BME backgrounds

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:19.620
in the London ambulance service earlier this
week. And hearing the story of people who

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:25.590
worked with the NHS in some cases for 30 years
who felt that they had been invisible throughout

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:31.170
that time, just brought it home to me how
much more work that we need to do to really

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:38.569
recognise and motivate people from the BME
backgrounds, and how much of contribution

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:43.030
- how much more of a contribution that they
can make. So those are just the three, sort

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.859
of little snapshot.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I can take straight from your

00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:55.469
third and maybe just add a few other thoughts
from my last few months. So yesterday, I was

00:03:55.469 --> 00:04:02.739
at a UK-India week conference health forum,
where actually, David, the words you've just

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:09.489
used, a BME consultant used exactly the same.
That she felt that she had been invisible.

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:14.260
So, we have both been hearing the same feedback
and I know we both care deeply about it. I've

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:19.800
spent a lot of time in the last six months
leading the work to develop the interim people

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:27.419
plan, which was published a few weeks ago.
Special thanks to David Behan in his companionship

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:33.560
and cooperation as we've developed that together,
and to Julian Hartley who presented at the

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:38.419
last joint board meeting. That work is really
only just beginning as we have set out the

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:44.669
interim people plan and the commitment to
produce a full people plan within a couple

00:04:44.669 --> 00:04:51.889
of months of the spending review and by the
end of the year regardless. And making the

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:57.990
NHS the best place to work across the country
is the overriding theme of that, and there's

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:02.370
no doubt we will only do that if we make it
an inclusive place for everyone who wants

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:07.060
to work in health and social care. So, a large
amount of my time has been devoted to that.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.750
And then, as I've been getting out and about,
I am increasingly trying to spend more time

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:18.479
shadowing people. And so I had a fascinating
day shadowing the physician's response unit

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:27.930
that work out of the Royal London. And this
is a that is staffed by a doctor and a paramedic,

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.510
and they are working in collaboration with
the Royal London, the London Ambulance Service

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:38.099
and the Air Ambulance Service. And it was
completely extraordinary to see what they

00:05:38.099 --> 00:05:45.070
are able to do in patients' homes. And this
is so in keeping and in tune with the direction

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:50.750
of travel of the long-term plan. Pushing carer
out to people's homes. And it was a brilliant

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:55.750
lesson in innovative new models of care that
are being tested and developed across the

00:05:55.750 --> 00:06:01.231
country. So that's just a snapshot from me
of one of the things I've been doing. Any

00:06:01.231 --> 00:06:09.409
questions or comments on David's and my report?
So, chief exec's report. Ian? Do you want

00:06:09.409 --> 00:06:14.979
to go first, Ian?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: Just two very quick things from

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:21.139
me, really. First, I'm pleased to be able
to publish the end of year report for the

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:28.330
NHS providers sector in the last couple of
weeks, looking at the achievements, the considerable

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:34.770
achievements, I think of the 1.1 million staff
who work in our 226 provider organisations.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:41.349
Too much in that a highlight in general. Just
a couple of very quick points, I think. First,

00:06:41.349 --> 00:06:47.669
despite recording once again record demand
for hospital, community and mental health

00:06:47.669 --> 00:06:53.009
services which absolutely were recorded, I
think particularly gratifying to see more

00:06:53.009 --> 00:06:58.189
patients being treated within national standards
than ever before, the first winter on winter

00:06:58.189 --> 00:07:05.870
improvement in core performance in five years.
The number of 52-week waiters reduced by over

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:13.250
63%, and the significant improvement in the
financial performance of the providers sector.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:18.800
So, I think it is not the case of course that
there is not more to do and the long-term

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:25.259
plan will be critical in that, but I think
it is worth just thanking the 1.1 million

00:07:25.259 --> 00:07:32.139
staff and their leadership for what I think
under extraordinary circumstances in terms

00:07:32.139 --> 00:07:39.419
of demand was actually very strong performance
last year and one that in many respects was

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:44.730
stronger than the previous few years. So I
think that is a massive testament to the quality

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:49.550
of leadership and front-line delivery across
our providers sector. And so I was happy to

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:57.979
present that to the public and to our staff.
And just for the record, and massive congratulations

00:07:57.979 --> 00:08:05.719
to Kettering General Hospital. It's always
really gratifying when a trust that is in

00:08:05.719 --> 00:08:12.520
special measures and has therefore got particular
challenges exits, and they were successful

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:17.830
in exiting quality special measures in May,
and I think that's an important testament

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:23.830
to the fact that real work has been done there
to ensure that the services patients get are

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:27.719
improved, and at the end of the day, that
is what it is about. So I think it's just

00:08:27.719 --> 00:08:31.960
worth always mentioning that at this point
as well. I think that's probably enough from

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:33.860
me, Dido. Happy to hand over to Simon at this
point.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:35.930
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Fantastic. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, thank you, Ian, and

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:39.810
let me just add my thanks to Ian for his leadership
and partnership in his stewardship of NHS

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:44.980
improvement and before that. I think it's
been a very productive period of the coming

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:53.770
together of two organisations, and I've greatly
enjoyed the opportunity to work with Ian.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:58.790
The same is true for my partnership with Matthew,
who, as you said, David has been leading NHS

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:04.280
England for three years now, and for all the
big changes that have been underway for the

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:08.670
last three years has been an instrumental
part of that, whether it's driving the improvements

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.570
on the information technology agenda, the
oversight of operational performance, and

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:19.260
really being the sort of spiritual driver
of a lot of the move towards integrated care

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:21.740
and population health which is now sort of
hardwired into what we're trying to do with

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:25.910
the long-term plan. So I'm immensely grateful
to Matthew as well for his personal leadership

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:33.900
and everything that he's done for the NHS
and for NHS England over that period.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:38.970
Obviously, those thoughts remind us that the
whole of the two organisations are in the

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:44.070
process of very considerable change and we
are right in the middle of that as we speak.

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:49.560
Yes, we've gone through the first two phases
of the organisational redesign, but actually

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:55.770
the bulk of our staff are now about to be
affected by the change process. We will be

00:09:55.770 --> 00:10:03.110
going through a public staff consultation
in July and August with a lot of people affected

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:08.510
September, October and November. As part of
that, I've been spending a lot of time over

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:13.840
the last six weeks out with our staff from
NHS improvement and NHS England and local

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:19.450
offices across the country. 13 different visits
over that period of time, hearing direct from

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:26.720
our people in places like Derby and Liverpool,
Southampton, Leeds, London, Rotherham, Manchester,

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:31.430
Lincoln, Doncaster, Brighton, to come along
with Houghton and the East Midlands. And I

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:37.210
think we just have to acknowledge as to boards
that that is kind of the state of play across

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:44.540
the organisation as a whole. Emily may want
to, if you come in in a moment, But at the

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:48.750
same time as we are grappling with some real
operational pressures which are on the agenda

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:52.370
today and it's vitally important that the
health service keeps that focus, and we're

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:56.470
kicking off the process with the long-term
plan implementation, I think as two boards,

00:10:56.470 --> 00:11:02.540
let's just remember what our own staff are
going through through this period.

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:05.590
If I can maybe just mentioned three other
things briefly but aren't otherwise covered

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:12.620
on the agenda. The first is that picking up
your point, Dido, on the work with our staff,

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:16.930
people across the NHS as a whole, just to
record that the junior doctors' committee

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:24.360
of the BMA have now voted in favour of a new
junior doctors' pay settlement which ends

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:31.410
the period of disagreement between themselves
and government. What that means is that we

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:37.430
go into the next several years with not only
the junior doctors on a multi-year settlement,

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:43.000
but of course the bulk of our staff through
Agenda for Change, and, as we know, the GPs

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:47.930
through the five-year contract. So, that provides
additional certainty for the whole of the

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:55.110
health service as we go into the LTP implementation
process, which I think our boards would welcome.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:12:01.560
Secondly, we have seen on Monday of this week
the response of the health select committee

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:07.690
to the legislative proposals which the boards
have previously discussed, and we very much

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:13.470
welcome the strong support that they have
given for the thrust of the proposals that

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:16.280
we have set out. That is obviously a discussion
that we'll be taking forward with the new

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:24.340
administration from the end of July.
And thirdly, just to make the obvious point

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:31.450
that we are back into a period of some uncertainty
as to Brexit, and so I took the opportunity

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:37.290
at the health select committee earlier in
the week to make the point that, whereas the

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:44.280
NHS had very effectively mobilised for the
possibility of a March 29th or indeed April

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:50.760
12th Brexit, those plans were put on hold
by government on April 26th, and if we are

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:57.720
collectively going to be ready again for October
31st, then it's very important that the transport

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:02.730
infrastructure that we are completely dependent
on, that is put in place. And in fact, since

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:08.260
saying that earlier in the week, I am pleased
that the Cabinet office have now initiated

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:12.840
the beginning of that procurement process
that we will clearly rely on.

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:21.420
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thanks, Simon. Any questions
or comments? I should say, and forgive me

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:25.040
Michael for not introducing you at the beginning,
I should actually - we haven't just acquired

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.720
a new non-executive director, but we are joined
as an observer by Michael [inaudible], who

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:33.630
is one of the non-executive directors at the
Department of Health and Social Care, who

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:37.970
is marking our homework today.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Well in that case, I'd better ask

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:49.030
a question, then. I make it an intelligent
one, which might be a bit of a struggle. [Inaudible]

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:55.930
reply after all. Just on Brexit, Simon. We
are now in the end of June and we could come

00:13:55.930 --> 00:14:02.020
out with a hard Brexit at the end of October.
I mean timewise in terms of revamping the

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:07.030
plans again, are you comfortable that that's
going to happen in time?

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:11.080
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, the infrastructure
that Keith will attend the team that was put

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:16.210
in place for March 29th, that infrastructure
is still there but will need to be reactivated.

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:23.010
And as Keith and I were discussing earlier
on in the week, by the time we had got the

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:31.400
run up to March 29th, all 400 also NHS organisations
which are being scrutinised were either green

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:41.040
or amber with a plan to get green from March
29th. We will need to go out and restart that

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:45.120
assurance process. There are some particular
issues that government will need to resolve

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:52.230
around stockpiling and what the request is
of supply chains, both the health specific

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:57.640
and the general. And again, as I've said early
on in the week, we've got different warehousing

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:05.060
and supply requirements over the winter season
then we would have had going into the Easter

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:10.590
period. But, look, the position of the health
service always has been, it is not our job

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:17.310
to do anything other than prepare to the greatest
extent that we possibly can so that people

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:21.200
continue to get the support they need. In
doing that, being explicit that there are

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:24.990
some dependencies that we have on others,
and helping make sure other people clock that

00:15:24.990 --> 00:16:02.280
fact and take the necessary action.
[BREAK IN AUDIO]

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:15.530
&gt;&gt;Speaker: So, having done a review of previous
phases, we've put more support in place for

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:21.770
colleagues in several ways. One is to make
sure there's an easier way to access expert

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:28.020
HR support, so people can ask questions in
confidence about what affects them. We've

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:33.990
also invested a bit in helping the two organisations
that have come together understand each other,

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:38.710
so there's quite a lot of places where the
organisations didn't really fully understand

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:42.270
the expert functions that were being carried
out. So, for example, putting in place a series

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:46.150
of Webexes that people can dial into, and
these are then recorded so people can download

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:50.060
them in their own time to understand some
of the complex functions the organisations

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:54.620
take place. And we've tried to make sure we're
both increasing the broadcast information,

00:16:54.620 --> 00:17:00.310
so that people get regular updates, but also
so that people can provide feedback and answer

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:06.459
questions. So we have a series of frequently
asked questions which are being updated on

00:17:06.459 --> 00:17:12.169
a rolling basis. And wherever possible, we
are doing things as one organisation, so for

00:17:12.169 --> 00:17:16.939
example, on the staff survey, rather than
deciding either improvement or the NHS England

00:17:16.939 --> 00:17:21.990
staff surveys are correct, we've got a working
group of colleagues putting together a robust

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:26.659
new staff survey which is based on obviously
data about what makes a good staff survey

00:17:26.659 --> 00:17:28.990
but also learnings from both organisations
that will then be rolled out as our new joint

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:34.049
staff survey in the autumn. So, making sure
as we go we are trying to make the organisation

00:17:34.049 --> 00:17:40.700
better to support colleagues as they go through
what is obviously a really difficult period

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:44.559
as well.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Great. Thank you. David?

00:17:44.559 --> 00:17:51.000
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Simon, if I could just connect
those three or four things. You've got management

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:59.570
change, you've got a big external event that
will be quite consuming, you've got very significant

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:03.240
change within the organisation, and you've
also got the long-term plan and the small

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:14.350
matter of delivery on performance. I'm interested
in knowing how you are thinking of balancing

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:21.299
and prioritising those competing claims on
the leadership team and on the organisation

00:18:21.299 --> 00:18:24.909
as a whole?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yeah. Well, obviously, most

00:18:24.909 --> 00:18:28.110
of those are not circumstances of our choosing,
but therefore as you say we've got to decide

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:35.230
how we respond to those multiple demands on
the organisation's work. We are going to get

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:40.019
onto the discussion on the long-term plan
and implementation. We are heavily relying

00:18:40.019 --> 00:18:47.520
and expecting that the work on that will principally
be led locally across the health service by

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:56.970
trust CCGs and STPs.
On the question of the engagement model between

00:18:56.970 --> 00:19:01.850
us as a new organisation or a combined organisation
and this local systems, that is one of the

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:06.340
things that we are signalling we want to work
with frontline leaders on, because there's

00:19:06.340 --> 00:19:10.309
got to be something different than what has
preceded, keeping the best of what has gone

00:19:10.309 --> 00:19:16.340
before but getting rid of things that need
to change. So, for our regional teams, that

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:18.240
is kind of going to be their lived reality,
having to make those juxtapositions. But the

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:23.340
truth is, we can't all do that as NHS England
and NHS improvement. This is something that

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:27.830
will have to be a shared responsibility with
leaders across the health service, and that

00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:31.120
is part of the reason why Ian and I and colleagues
has been spending so much time, and I personally

00:19:31.120 --> 00:19:36.399
have been spending so much time directly with
trust chief executives and the CCG leaders

00:19:36.399 --> 00:19:42.909
over the last six weeks engaging and hearing
from them on how best we do what you have

00:19:42.909 --> 00:19:46.720
asked.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: And are there pinch points in the

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:47.720
regions?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, there are certainly

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:51.529
some pinch points in the regions, but if you
- without sort of re-figuring the discussion

00:19:51.529 --> 00:19:57.410
we're going to have on the LTP, we've agreed
with chief execs across the service that there

00:19:57.410 --> 00:20:02.190
are some big items, whether it's the trust
financial regime, the new capital regime,

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:06.360
the redesign of outpatients and so on, that
we're going to work direct with our leaders

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:11.390
across the health service on, not sort of
simply leaving that to the regions to try

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:18.320
and figure out. But you're right - I mean,
you are fundamentally right to point the multiple

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.770
stressors bearing down on the organisation
as a time of enormous transition.

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:28.120
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: This probably sets the scene
very nicely for the next couple of agenda

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:29.779
items, I think. If everyone's happy, we'll
move on. Julian, are you going to -

00:20:29.779 --> 00:20:33.450
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: I think we're going to kick
off with Pauline and Matthew and then I'll

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:38.330
come to the financial side. Sorry about that.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay. Got it. That's all right.

00:20:38.330 --> 00:20:42.050
Keeping me on my toes. Pauline, are you starting?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes, okay. So, I think during

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:51.460
our last meeting I indicated that performance
from an emergency care point of view during

00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:57.499
this winter had been better than the previous
winter, and this report starts off by confirming

00:20:57.499 --> 00:21:03.830
that situation, not just for the winter but
then looking at the total number of additional

00:21:03.830 --> 00:21:09.290
patients that we have been able to see that
we were able to see during four hours for

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:15.399
the whole of last year, which I think was
about 800,000, and during the winter period

00:21:15.399 --> 00:21:22.309
it was 380,000. So I think a significant increase
in the number of patients seen within four

00:21:22.309 --> 00:21:31.690
hours for winter proper, higher for our performance
in spite of all despite the increase in demand

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:38.610
that Ian referred to earlier. I think importantly
for last year and indeed for winter, we saw

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:44.450
that real improvements in some of the metrics
that mean an awful lot to patients around

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:51.139
12 our trolley waits, around weights in the
back of ambulances when they arrive at hospital,

00:21:51.139 --> 00:21:56.970
et cetera, et cetera. So I think again, as
we said at the last meeting, that was very

00:21:56.970 --> 00:22:04.009
much down to the hard work of our staff day
in day out, and we've seen that work continue

00:22:04.009 --> 00:22:08.710
as we faced some new operational issues coming
into this year.

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:17.390
The second thing that we covered at our last
meeting was looking at demand and what was

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:24.899
behind the demand numbers, and we explained
that we were doing a deep dive exercise to

00:22:24.899 --> 00:22:31.549
understand, especially around attendances,
the 7.8% increase, and what had caused that

00:22:31.549 --> 00:22:37.409
over the February to April period. Again,
it set out in the paper so I'm not going to

00:22:37.409 --> 00:22:42.840
go over it in detail, but you will see that
it's a combination of starting with a lower

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:50.759
base from the previous year, because this
7.8 is essentially made up of minors and patients

00:22:50.759 --> 00:22:57.909
with minor conditions tended not to arrive
in emergency departments in the same numbers

00:22:57.909 --> 00:23:02.210
in our previous winter because of the cold
period and because of other perceptions that

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:07.970
they may have had about how quickly they would
have been cared for. But I think really importantly

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:15.070
to say that as part of those demand numbers,
the very positive reform agenda that we put

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:21.779
in place has contributed to where the numbers
are being counted. In particular, around the

00:23:21.779 --> 00:23:30.659
urgent treatment centres and the scaling up
of GP streaming, et cetera.

00:23:30.659 --> 00:23:36.360
With regards to admissions, we have previously
explained the fact that same day emergency

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:43.879
care has grown significantly in the last year
and indeed we are encouraging its ongoing

00:23:43.879 --> 00:23:51.360
development and that does account for a significant
impact on what we've seen in the admissions

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:58.501
growth last year. And again, that's set out
in the paper.

00:23:58.501 --> 00:24:02.049
Before we sort of leave last year, I think
just one sort of final comment to make on

00:24:02.049 --> 00:24:09.929
it and on winter, we talked a lot over the
winter period about the contribution of local

00:24:09.929 --> 00:24:15.019
authorities and that we were getting very
positive feedback from our local services,

00:24:15.019 --> 00:24:22.710
from our local systems around the joint work
that had taken place, and from the data that

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:28.419
has now been made available to us, we can
see the hard numbers behind that, and the

00:24:28.419 --> 00:24:33.120
real impact that has had on supporting us
in managing through the winter and for winter

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:39.710
being a very different experience, we've got
details about the care packages that were

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:45.429
purchased, the care at home, et cetera, et
cetera.

00:24:45.429 --> 00:24:50.619
The paper then moves on to talk about the
ongoing work on transformation, which is clearly

00:24:50.619 --> 00:24:57.730
very important to us at the moment, but basically,
we set out here that the progress achieved

00:24:57.730 --> 00:25:04.539
last year as far as length of stay was concerned
continues into this year. The same for DTalk[?]

00:25:04.539 --> 00:25:09.779
and we give a little bit more information
about the ongoing work of the ambulance service

00:25:09.779 --> 00:25:14.299
and 111, where the transformation work continues
to be successful.

00:25:14.299 --> 00:25:23.779
Finally, we mention 111 online, where we now
have 400% rollout, but really importantly,

00:25:23.779 --> 00:25:29.440
greater coverage of the phase three part of
that which is the total service to about 81.8%

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:36.280
of the population at the moment.
If it's okay, I'll just move to RTT straight

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:45.570
away. So, again, looking at our RTT performance
the last year, and just highlighting a couple

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:53.101
of issues there, I think the first one to
highlight is the additional - the total number

00:25:53.101 --> 00:25:59.450
of patients who been seen during the 18-week
period last year. And we've seen an edition

00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:06.619
of 14.3 million, which I think is one of the
highest increases over the previous years.

00:26:06.619 --> 00:26:13.960
Again, down to a concerted effort from all
of our staff. Ian mentioned in his report

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:21.230
the excellent work that was done to support
patients not to have long waits. The 52 week

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:27.779
wait issue. And the up-to-date information
suggests that although the mandate last year

00:26:27.779 --> 00:26:36.559
was to reduce that by 50%, by now we have
reduced it by 70.2% over the peak that was

00:26:36.559 --> 00:26:44.149
last June. And again, we've highlighted a
couple of the transformation initiatives that

00:26:44.149 --> 00:26:49.519
are playing a fundamental part in all of that
work. That's it from me.

00:26:49.519 --> 00:26:53.690
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Fantastic. Matthew, do you
want to add on that before we open it up?

00:26:53.690 --> 00:27:01.659
&gt;&gt;Matthew Tagney: So, I'll touch on some of
the other areas of performance. So, I think

00:27:01.659 --> 00:27:07.649
it's probably first just worth just making
the point, and following on from Pauline,

00:27:07.649 --> 00:27:13.419
that the A&amp;E departments are working under
a lot of pressure at the moment but we have

00:27:13.419 --> 00:27:18.220
a range of other routes to get the support
you need which don't involve going to an A&amp;E

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:23.399
department. But if you are living around here
in the Elephant and Castle, your options include

00:27:23.399 --> 00:27:29.330
ringing 111, were 90% of people would get
an answer within 60 seconds, to talk to somebody,

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:34.239
and half of those people will end up speaking
to a position, or they could go to the walk-in

00:27:34.239 --> 00:27:40.999
centre at Guys Hospital, where 99% would be
seen within four hours and would get the appropriate

00:27:40.999 --> 00:27:44.830
care they needed a whole lot quicker, and
leave the St Thomas' A&amp;E department or the

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:49.120
King's A&amp;E department for the people who actually
need the expert support that a major acute

00:27:49.120 --> 00:27:55.309
hospital has. So, do yourself a favour and
do the NHS a favour by using the services

00:27:55.309 --> 00:28:02.390
properly. And I think those other services
are increasingly becoming effective and important.

00:28:02.390 --> 00:28:08.340
Touching on, just a couple of other areas,
I think where we were talking about in cancer,

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:13.409
people will see that the cancer performance
in particular at 62 days, not where we wanted

00:28:13.409 --> 00:28:21.740
to be. We saw last year a 16% increase in
referrals into our cancer services. That is

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:25.649
good news. We are identifying more cancers
than we ever have before, which means we are

00:28:25.649 --> 00:28:33.559
able to treat people more quickly than we
ever have before. We saw an 11% increase in

00:28:33.559 --> 00:28:39.909
the number of patients being seen and treated
within 62 days. So that is a tremendous performance

00:28:39.909 --> 00:28:46.140
by the NHS. However, the challenge that we
have set the NHS this year is, okay, we now

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:50.619
know that in our messaging about, come forward
if you think you have a cancer problem, come

00:28:50.619 --> 00:28:55.070
forward and make sure that you access the
services swiftly. That message is coming through

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:58.330
and people are coming forward. Now it is up
to the NHS to put the extra capacity in place

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:05.300
to make sure that all of those people are
receiving care as quickly as is deemed clinically

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:11.370
appropriate. And there is a lot of work now
going on across the NHS to say, we now need

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:15.179
to put on the extra outpatient clinics, the
extra diagnostic services and the extra surgical

00:29:15.179 --> 00:29:21.049
services in order to ensure that everybody
gets treated quicker. So, I think that's a

00:29:21.049 --> 00:29:25.440
major piece of work that we need in order
to get ourselves not only seen and treating

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:30.110
more cancers more quickly but actually hitting
the target that we set ourselves, which we

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:35.009
are not at the moment.
In terms of the new policy of the roll-out

00:29:35.009 --> 00:29:41.639
of primary care networks, we have almost 100%
coverage. We have 98% coverage of GP practices

00:29:41.639 --> 00:29:47.220
now align to a primary care network. That's
a tremendous step forward in terms of creating

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:56.100
the platform for joined up, integrated care
for local people, and that work goes on. And

00:29:56.100 --> 00:30:03.369
in the same vein, we've announced in the last
few days the next three integrated care systems

00:30:03.369 --> 00:30:09.581
in Buckinghamshire, Oxfordshire, and Berkshire
West. Buckinghamshire and Berkshire West were

00:30:09.581 --> 00:30:14.509
already integrated systems. Oxfordshire has
joined them to make one population group for

00:30:14.509 --> 00:30:21.600
working together with greater integrated care.
North East and North Cumbria is a journey

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:28.879
of real success. That has gone from five years
ago being one of our most challenged and most

00:30:28.879 --> 00:30:34.510
struggling health systems in special measures
regime and close intervention to now being

00:30:34.510 --> 00:30:39.759
one of our best performing and most closely
tightly knit health systems in the country.

00:30:39.759 --> 00:30:45.740
And all tribute to them. And lastly, south-east
London is the first of our London FTPs to

00:30:45.740 --> 00:30:54.330
move into being an integrated care system.
So I think there's real progress there. We

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:58.950
are continuing to hit and be on track for
all our mental health targets, so just the

00:30:58.950 --> 00:31:02.940
last thing I would like to touch on is learning
disability and autism. As I hand this role

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:08.529
over to Amanda Pritchett when she comes in
to take it on, I hope the board and Amanda

00:31:08.529 --> 00:31:13.830
will give as much focus to learning disabilities
and autism as we have tried to over the last

00:31:13.830 --> 00:31:19.989
several years. The number of people with learning
disabilities in an inpatient setting has fallen

00:31:19.989 --> 00:31:27.009
by 22% over the last few years, but our target
is to halve that number. There is a lot of

00:31:27.009 --> 00:31:32.450
work to be done and perhaps just as importantly,
the work that we've started to look at the

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:35.279
whole of the pathway, from, does every GP
know the patients who have a learning disability

00:31:35.279 --> 00:31:41.340
or autism who are on their list, through to
before we make a decision, to admit anyone

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:48.590
to hospital, is all the support in place to
avoid that, right through to, are we focused

00:31:48.590 --> 00:31:51.850
on getting the right packages of care and
the right support in the community to get

00:31:51.850 --> 00:31:56.120
people back out of hospital and living an
independent life, and when something goes

00:31:56.120 --> 00:32:01.299
wrong, do we investigate and learn from that?
There is an entire pathway that we are working

00:32:01.299 --> 00:32:05.649
so hard to put in place, and I think I'll
ask the board just to support that we should

00:32:05.649 --> 00:32:09.100
continue to focus on that that was laid out
in the five-year forward review and repeated

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:13.720
in the long-term plan and I think really is
a step forward for some of our most vulnerable

00:32:13.720 --> 00:32:20.340
people in the NHS's delivery.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Absolutely. Julian, do you

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:23.249
want to come next and then we'll open up?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: Yeah, I mean so, just on the

00:32:23.249 --> 00:32:28.659
finances, the paper sets out the position
really at the close of 2018/2019, and Ian

00:32:28.659 --> 00:32:38.009
has already referred to the provider sector
position which had a total sector adjusted

00:32:38.009 --> 00:32:48.360
deficit of about £571 million. That is offset
for the whole of the NHS on the commissioner

00:32:48.360 --> 00:32:56.739
side with an underspend relative to plan of
£916 million. When that sort of aggregates

00:32:56.739 --> 00:33:02.600
at the Department of Health level, there are
some ins and outs. The biggest out is fundamentally

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:10.769
the transaction on Carillion, which of course
is a credit in this sector adjusted side.

00:33:10.769 --> 00:33:18.520
So, to the Department of Health, the NHS position
basically comes in on an £89 million underspend,

00:33:18.520 --> 00:33:30.580
which is quite a feat on £113 billion of
spend and I also need to congratulate the

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:40.940
teams who succeed on achieving that result.
Worth saying on the capital side that in aggregate,

00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:46.749
providers basically underspend against their
plans to achieve about £300 million. That

00:33:46.749 --> 00:33:53.500
is different to then how the Department of
Health again looks at it, who sort of settled

00:33:53.500 --> 00:34:02.519
allocation for the NHS, and relative to that
allocation, the overspend is about £350 million.

00:34:02.519 --> 00:34:08.490
Al just dwell on that just for one minute,
because although providers underspent against

00:34:08.490 --> 00:34:14.919
plans, the underspend in 2018/2019 relative
to previous years was much lower. Actually,

00:34:14.919 --> 00:34:18.120
the slippage from what they said they were
going to spend to what they spend is much

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:24.390
lower. And it probably indicates both that
actually people are getting better at doing

00:34:24.390 --> 00:34:29.481
what they said they were going to do, and
indeed some of the pent-up demand that is

00:34:29.481 --> 00:34:34.910
in the system to address maintenance issues
which we of course will need to be thinking

00:34:34.910 --> 00:34:40.390
about as we look forward to the future. That's
probably enough.

00:34:40.390 --> 00:34:43.060
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Fantastic. Thank you. Joanne,
do you want to go?

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:46.470
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Yeah, it was actually just
following up from Pauline and Matthew, there's

00:34:46.470 --> 00:34:53.850
a comment on the question. So the comment
was, looking at Tim here, in our quality committee

00:34:53.850 --> 00:34:58.840
yesterday, I think we spent the bulk of our
time looking at that learning disability work

00:34:58.840 --> 00:35:05.260
all the way through to mortality review and
the pathway that you describe, and an amazing

00:35:05.260 --> 00:35:10.700
video describing the experience of a person
with learning disabilities in hospital. So

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:14.250
I just wanted to echo Matthew's point. I think
the works been extraordinary. The challenges

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:23.340
are huge, and I'm sure we will continue to
focus on the. So that was a comment. Really,

00:35:23.340 --> 00:35:28.840
really worthwhile work.
And the question was about, it's really good

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:33.770
to see 111 online available everywhere. I
wondered whether we were able yet to have

00:35:33.770 --> 00:35:42.640
a feel for the impact that ubiquitous access
to 111 online and the upping of clinical support

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:51.390
on the 111 phone service, actually what impact
that was having on reducing unnecessary attendances

00:35:51.390 --> 00:35:55.440
to face-to-face care? Do we know?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Tagney: Yes. So, we have - we have

00:35:55.440 --> 00:36:02.660
a system which allows us to track what advice
we give to people at 111 and what they actually

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:08.180
do, to the extent that if we suggest that
they go to a pharmacy and they turn up at

00:36:08.180 --> 00:36:13.400
the A&amp;E department, we can see. So we can
see that as we've increased the clinical input,

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:21.810
people taking the advice from 111 has improved.
There's still a way to go, and there's still

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:27.220
an extent to which A&amp;Es are very trusted and
people just end up going there, but there's

00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:33.230
no question: they are diverted people away
from A&amp;E. And the acid test of that was last

00:36:33.230 --> 00:36:39.510
winter, where in places where the A&amp;E - where
111 was under pressure, the A&amp;Es had started

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:44.320
to complain that one-on-one wasn't relieving
pressure on them, so I think, Pauline, if

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:48.000
you want to add to that?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: No, I completely agree,

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:55.040
and we do monitor very carefully the roll-out
of 111 and we will be doing likewise with

00:36:55.040 --> 00:37:01.310
111 online to check the conversion rate as
Matthew said, to see if people do what we

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:06.910
advise them to do.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Thank you. Really, for Matthew

00:37:06.910 --> 00:37:12.460
and Pauline, notwithstanding some difficult
circumstances and increased demand in a number

00:37:12.460 --> 00:37:17.130
of areas, you've touched upon A&amp;E and cancer,
what's your sense of confidence about improvement

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:24.950
and when and how that will happen in those
two areas?

00:37:24.950 --> 00:37:31.780
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: I think to start off by
saying that this winter has been a very good

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:37.090
experience for staff in the NHS. They have
seen the power of transformation, and they

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:43.360
have seen that it gives patients a much better
experience at the end of the day. The service

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:49.590
is out of hospital, how they have linked into
the front of the hospital, et cetera, et cetera.

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:57.740
And I think that commitment from the total
system to understanding that the hospital

00:37:57.740 --> 00:38:02.920
can't operate in isolation, but that it's
not just about the transformation agenda,

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:10.060
it's about learning new ways of delivering
services and new approaches to improvement.

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:16.310
And so I think there's an air of confidence
at this point. Clearly, we got some operational

00:38:16.310 --> 00:38:20.920
challenges at the moment, and we'd need to
be able to see if we can go further, faster,

00:38:20.920 --> 00:38:26.840
and keep that confidence and to keep that
goodwill.

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:36.160
&gt;&gt;Matthew Tagney: On cancer, I think there
are ways we can improve very quickly, and

00:38:36.160 --> 00:38:42.760
some things which are going to take a bit
longer. So, when we look - firstly, we see

00:38:42.760 --> 00:38:48.190
variation between different providers confronted
by the same challenges. And we see in most

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:54.510
places that a patient fed directly to a cancer
centre is being seen very quickly. It's the

00:38:54.510 --> 00:39:00.200
longer cancer alliance. It's where you go
into one [inaudible] they're referring you

00:39:00.200 --> 00:39:03.110
to another hospital that there is a slowdown.
And therefore, those are the things which

00:39:03.110 --> 00:39:07.900
managerial we can fix, because we have the
right people, patients are receiving care,

00:39:07.900 --> 00:39:13.090
it just takes too long. And we have put more
money into the system in order to invest in

00:39:13.090 --> 00:39:17.930
extra capacity. So, I have no doubt that we
can get quite significant improvement quite

00:39:17.930 --> 00:39:24.900
quickly. There is also, though, a genuine
capacity problem about the right number of

00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:30.880
the right skilled people, be they endoscopists
or be they cancer nurses, to run some of those

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:37.050
services. And we need to make sure that our
recruitment and training of staff and the

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:42.620
changes in working practices so that we use
people at what the Americans call the limit

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:47.890
of their licence, but actually getting the
best skills out of all our people, is going

00:39:47.890 --> 00:39:53.680
to be necessary as well. So some of those
things we need to be picking up as part of

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:56.410
a longer-term plan. Other things, we need
to just get on and do really well now.

00:39:56.410 --> 00:40:02.060
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Ruth, and maybe Steve, give
us a comment on how clinicians are feeling

00:40:02.060 --> 00:40:08.000
and would reflect on performance.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: I think it's all relative, to

00:40:08.000 --> 00:40:12.820
be honest, this last winter compared to the
winter before. We know with 39,500 registered

00:40:12.820 --> 00:40:18.660
nursing vacancies across England and the provider
sector that things are tough. Things are really

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:22.910
tough out there. Although we are doing what
we can, I know trusts across the country are

00:40:22.910 --> 00:40:29.590
making huge strides in their retention collaboratives
and retaining more and more staff, being much

00:40:29.590 --> 00:40:34.500
more flexible, and we are equally rolling
out our retention programme, particularly

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:39.210
focusing on those A&amp;Es, the most challenged
A&amp;Es, we are putting more resources into that.

00:40:39.210 --> 00:40:46.750
But it is tough out there for nurses. I'm
sure Steve will want to comment from a wider

00:40:46.750 --> 00:40:50.740
clinical point of view. And that's why am
delighted that as part of the interim people

00:40:50.740 --> 00:40:55.910
plan we are prioritising the nursing work
force is the area that we're going to focus

00:40:55.910 --> 00:41:00.920
on. Because we do need to address the missing
nurses, midwives and care workers if we are

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:06.330
going to be able to provide safe care consistently
along that pathway. And I know with colleagues

00:41:06.330 --> 00:41:12.790
in HE and indeed every director of nursing
in England, we will want to make sure that

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:21.830
the nurses' life day in day out is an improved
one as a result of the interim people plan.

00:41:21.830 --> 00:41:24.030
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes, I'd maybe reflect a few
things. So, like Simon and everybody around

00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:28.730
this room, I spend a lot of time going out
and visiting. So in the last few months I've

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:32.370
been everywhere from Carlisle in the north
to the Isle of Wight in the south. I've been

00:41:32.370 --> 00:41:35.060
in the A&amp;E department in Whitehaven, and in
the A&amp;E department in Margate, from coast

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:41.140
to coast. And I think I reflect on what Matthew
and Pauline have said.

00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:47.090
So, firstly, I think during the winter months,
I think the feeling was certainly among staff

00:41:47.090 --> 00:41:54.170
that it was a better winter than last year,
and I think that's something that I absolutely

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:57.000
heard when we were in the depths of winter,
which doesn't feel so very long ago. That's

00:41:57.000 --> 00:41:59.380
the first thing.
The second thing that I would say is that

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:03.040
staff are working incredibly hard and those
pressures remain, and like everybody around

00:42:03.040 --> 00:42:07.340
the room I'm really grateful for all the hard
work that all the clinical staff do, but of

00:42:07.340 --> 00:42:13.720
course as a doctor particularly, doctors and
emergency medicine staff and the acute positions

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:20.050
that sit behind that, and all the other specialists
that put that input in. And of course, our

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:26.030
general practice collies, because we shouldn't
forget the primary care is also doing a lot

00:42:26.030 --> 00:42:27.990
of work in this area.
And then the third thing I'd say is I continue

00:42:27.990 --> 00:42:31.020
to see enthusiasm for new models and new ways
of doing things. So, particularly on the same

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:36.140
day emergency care, I'm always struck with
where I go to the places have got in place

00:42:36.140 --> 00:42:41.670
plans to do that, and I saw that in action
in Margate last week. And so I think there

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:46.150
is a sense that there is a process of transformation
going on, and I think clinicians are really

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:48.680
up for doing that.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Right. Thank you. David, you

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:51.290
wanted to come in?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So, I think just to echo what

00:42:51.290 --> 00:42:57.780
Julian said is that I think the management
teams across the NHS did a wonderful job in

00:42:57.780 --> 00:43:03.810
landing the numbers where they did. However
in the category of unhelpful observations,

00:43:03.810 --> 00:43:11.270
lurking within the pack is a slide on efficiency,
which shows that whilst we did a great job

00:43:11.270 --> 00:43:17.590
in delivering 3.1 billion of efficiency savings,
that was 900 million - sorry, 2.2, that's

00:43:17.590 --> 00:43:26.110
900 million less than we'd hoped for, and
it was balanced by some over delivery on nonrecurrent

00:43:26.110 --> 00:43:34.920
savings, which just begs the question in the
environment that were going into this year

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:45.660
where there is less scope on the contingency
as to how confident we might feel about the

00:43:45.660 --> 00:43:49.490
1.1 to 1.5% efficiency plans and how rigorous
and well-founded they are, because they are

00:43:49.490 --> 00:43:51.230
quite critical.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Ian, do you want to comment

00:43:51.230 --> 00:43:54.540
on that? Ian, and then Julian.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: Yeah. I mean, I would say that

00:43:54.540 --> 00:44:01.150
there was significant efficiency delivered
last year, but we have seen a trend over the

00:44:01.150 --> 00:44:07.350
last few years whereby international standards,
stretching levels of efficiency are achieved,

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:13.890
but they fall short of what the NHS needs,
and that gap is typically made up by nonrecurrent

00:44:13.890 --> 00:44:21.470
measures. I think it is absolutely fair to
say that that does need to change. The plans

00:44:21.470 --> 00:44:28.220
that have been submitted for 2019/2020, I
think certainly on the provider side, which

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:36.350
is where we are really talking about, suggest
that I think compared to any previous year,

00:44:36.350 --> 00:44:40.890
the overwhelming majority of trusts have signed
up to their controlled totals, and have a

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:47.060
budget that their boards tellers that they
can achieve within, which was our goal. So

00:44:47.060 --> 00:44:51.810
out of 226, all but four have signed up to
their control totals. And that's really not

00:44:51.810 --> 00:44:59.060
been achieved before. But it is fair to say
that that reform journey to support that is

00:44:59.060 --> 00:45:04.900
dependent upon making those recurrent savings.
So, I think my senses that this would be something

00:45:04.900 --> 00:45:07.550
the board would want to continue to pay attention
to across the year.

00:45:07.550 --> 00:45:10.310
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I think there are two reasons.
One is the financial path but the second thing

00:45:10.310 --> 00:45:13.250
is that every pound of efficiency saving is
invested in patient care.

00:45:13.250 --> 00:45:19.980
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: And it's fair to say that every
pound is therefore caring for patients. And

00:45:19.980 --> 00:45:28.030
nonrecurrent measures are clearly not the
answer, because all that happens is that at

00:45:28.030 --> 00:45:31.120
the beginning of the next year you have to
do those and the efficiency you then didn't

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:40.210
do, and so I think, I don't at all decry the
efforts of NHS staff, but as I say, by any

00:45:40.210 --> 00:45:44.720
international standards, the NHS is making
significant efficiencies, but at the same

00:45:44.720 --> 00:45:50.880
time, that is a major priority for this year,
otherwise a financial settlement that we've

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:55.940
got won't work. I think Julian, if you want
to give any different opinion on the planners

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:57.670
themselves?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: As we completed the planning

00:45:57.670 --> 00:46:04.220
around, I mean the pluses are the kind of
alignment between commissioners and providers

00:46:04.220 --> 00:46:07.950
is much, much better than it was last year.
So the sort of the gaps that we've historically

00:46:07.950 --> 00:46:11.950
seen between contracts and plans are sort
of de minimis. Nonetheless, quite frankly,

00:46:11.950 --> 00:46:19.320
as we've spoken to the finance teams in the
regions and the providers and CCGs, actually,

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:25.810
it's like the one thing we are now focusing
on is, okay, where have you signed up to efficiency

00:46:25.810 --> 00:46:30.150
targets, and let's be really clear on where
you don't yet really - you haven't identified

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:36.450
the actions. And so the focus of effort is
already, and it will certainly be over the

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:44.580
next, well, the whole year, but at least the
next couple of months, identifying those systems,

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:50.460
those organisations, where there's not been
sufficient progress in identifying how they

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:52.150
are going to turn the number on the spreadsheet
into the actual action. So before I head off

00:46:52.150 --> 00:46:59.270
for my summer holidays, I've got a better
conference that that kind of number, which

00:46:59.270 --> 00:47:03.280
is fundamentally the biggest risk we face,
as you've just said, is a much smaller number

00:47:03.280 --> 00:47:10.190
than it even was at the point we concluded
the planning round. And then actually one

00:47:10.190 --> 00:47:13.660
of the reasons for concluding the planning
round was to stop just talking about it and

00:47:13.660 --> 00:47:15.000
then get into and now see how that's turning
into action.

00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:21.220
So, how comfortable am I? I don't think I
sit comfortably here today and I won't sit

00:47:21.220 --> 00:47:25.710
comfortably until, you know, there's real
actions behind it.

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:31.120
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Richard?
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: I just had a point of clarity

00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:37.930
for Julian again. So, you mentioned the capital
last year and the overspend against the sort

00:47:37.930 --> 00:47:43.640
of the allocation, and just to be clear, unless
my understanding is wrong, there isn't a capital

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:46.110
allocation to NHS providers, so I don't want
people to think this is a failure of governance.

00:47:46.110 --> 00:47:53.840
The way the system currently works means right
from the DH down to the provider, there isn't

00:47:53.840 --> 00:47:59.450
a capital budget, which is one of the reasons
why as part of the long-term plan changes,

00:47:59.450 --> 00:48:04.060
some legislative changes, we've got to make
some pretty significant changes to the capital

00:48:04.060 --> 00:48:09.981
regime. So that, although in DH's books they
will appear as £200 million issued, it's

00:48:09.981 --> 00:48:14.640
not a governance failure, because there wasn't
a budget against which to overspend.

00:48:14.640 --> 00:48:20.420
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: It is probably just was saying
that is why I described it as an allocation.

00:48:20.420 --> 00:48:22.850
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Very careful language.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: That's entirely correct.

00:48:22.850 --> 00:48:27.720
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I just like to make a comment

00:48:27.720 --> 00:48:33.520
in relation to the point that David made about
the efficiency gain, because we all know there's

00:48:33.520 --> 00:48:40.170
a lot more efficiency to go out in the NHS,
but over time, if we're going to get real

00:48:40.170 --> 00:48:46.740
productivity in place, were going to have
to spend some capital. You know, if CT machines

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:50.390
are breaking down because we can't replace
them or were still operating out of Victorian

00:48:50.390 --> 00:48:56.910
buildings because we can't do the new build,
were not going to get the productivity gains

00:48:56.910 --> 00:49:02.950
and the efficiency improvements. So I think,
you know, the revenue account of the capital

00:49:02.950 --> 00:49:06.570
are not wholly divorced from each other, and
I think, you know, we've got a five-year agreement

00:49:06.570 --> 00:49:10.480
or revenue but we've got to get something
- a matching agreement on capital as well.

00:49:10.480 --> 00:49:15.420
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: And just to build on that,
it's the physical capital, the digital capital,

00:49:15.420 --> 00:49:19.970
and the human capital, otherwise we won't
get the transformation that will deliver the

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:23.240
promise set out in the long-term plan.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I entirely agree.

00:49:23.240 --> 00:49:27.850
&gt;&gt;Speaker: And actually, I'm going to add
to that, and Simon had made the point about

00:49:27.850 --> 00:49:33.640
the stock of health or ill-health. You know,
if we don't invest in prevention, to reduce

00:49:33.640 --> 00:49:40.930
the stock of future ill-health, then that
is also building up, so it is also capital

00:49:40.930 --> 00:49:45.540
in that.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes. Yes. Any further comments

00:49:45.540 --> 00:49:55.250
or questions regarding operational measures?
Excellent. Thank you. In which case, we move

00:49:55.250 --> 00:50:02.200
on, Matthew, come up to the table to talk
us through where we are with the long-term

00:50:02.200 --> 00:50:05.110
plan implementation framework.
&gt;&gt;Matthew Tagney: Thank you very much, Dido.

00:50:05.110 --> 00:50:11.240
So, as the board knows, earlier this year
we published the long-term plan. In it, we

00:50:11.240 --> 00:50:16.850
set out that we would publish implementation
framework which would be used by ICSs and

00:50:16.850 --> 00:50:24.080
STPs over the summer to produce the strategic
plans covering from this year to 2023/2024.

00:50:24.080 --> 00:50:29.550
That those plans would be brought together
into a full national implementation plan by

00:50:29.550 --> 00:50:35.970
the end of the year, and that we would publish.
So, today, we are seeking the board's agreement

00:50:35.970 --> 00:50:43.190
and approval to publish the implementation
framework, which we have. The process of creating

00:50:43.190 --> 00:50:46.440
that framework was based on the significance
of massive engagement, and I think there were

00:50:46.440 --> 00:50:50.700
three key things that came back from our engagement
with systems, with national stakeholders,

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:57.190
with patient groups and with voluntary VCSE
organisations as well. The first was a genuine

00:50:57.190 --> 00:51:03.000
enthusiasm and desire to work with us to deliver
across the entire breadth of the long-term

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:08.490
plan and make sure we deliver across that
entire breadth. The second was a desire to

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:14.640
some support on sequencing of commitments
within the long-term plan, to identify what

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:19.780
are the things that we need to devote particular
attention to making progress on in the early

00:51:19.780 --> 00:51:25.810
years. And third was to - as our systems develop,
and continue to develop and grow a desire

00:51:25.810 --> 00:51:29.690
that they will want to use this plan to build
on existing strategies and partnerships they

00:51:29.690 --> 00:51:34.190
have in place and to reflect the particular
local needs and starting points of their systems

00:51:34.190 --> 00:51:38.880
as they do that.
Therefore, the first thing this document has

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:44.190
and seeks to do is to address those three
points, to make a clear request to the NHS

00:51:44.190 --> 00:51:50.840
to plan to deliver across the entire breadth
of the long-term plan, but also to acknowledge

00:51:50.840 --> 00:51:55.920
that the long-term plans set endpoints of
2023, 2024 for the majority of commitments.

00:51:55.920 --> 00:52:01.750
And for those commitments, we are asking systems
to define the trajectory working with the

00:52:01.750 --> 00:52:12.640
local partners to get the agreed endpoint.
For a minority of areas, we are seeking additional

00:52:12.640 --> 00:52:17.390
detail or setting additional trajectories,
particularly where that involves creating

00:52:17.390 --> 00:52:20.680
the capacity that we need to deliver on the
totality of the long-term plan, for example

00:52:20.680 --> 00:52:24.590
investments in primary community care and
the expansion of those services, or around

00:52:24.590 --> 00:52:29.100
meeting our mental health commitments.
The other area primarily will be looking at

00:52:29.100 --> 00:52:34.030
doing that is where it creates the structures
that we require to deliver, particularly around

00:52:34.030 --> 00:52:38.260
establishing and growing and maturing our
ICSs.

00:52:38.260 --> 00:52:42.030
The second thing this document does as it
seeks to set out some principles that we are

00:52:42.030 --> 00:52:47.030
asking the NHS to use when developing their
plans. The plans are clinically led, that

00:52:47.030 --> 00:52:55.490
they are locally owned, that they are built
on a good understanding of workforce requirements

00:52:55.490 --> 00:53:00.810
and that those requirements are realistic.
That they deliver the five financial tests

00:53:00.810 --> 00:53:04.771
set by government and deliver within the existing
resources available to systems, and that they

00:53:04.771 --> 00:53:10.000
place prevention and equality is right at
the heart of the planning process.

00:53:10.000 --> 00:53:17.150
The third thing the document does as it seeks
to support systems to do the strategic planning,

00:53:17.150 --> 00:53:22.550
and it does that by first of all giving additional
certainty to systems in addition to their

00:53:22.550 --> 00:53:27.430
five-year allocations they already have, indicative
allocations they have, on the service development

00:53:27.430 --> 00:53:31.270
funding that will be made available over the
five-year so they can plan within a specific

00:53:31.270 --> 00:53:36.750
financial envelope. And we've also as part
of that sought to make as much of that funding

00:53:36.750 --> 00:53:43.350
tied to the single process of agreeing their
plans to make it simpler and easier to then,

00:53:43.350 --> 00:53:47.760
for them based on feedback that we have received
from the plan.

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:52.460
The implementation framework sets out a small
number of metrics or measures, or starts to,

00:53:52.460 --> 00:54:00.060
where we will be seeking to measure and test
the implementation and test how implementation

00:54:00.060 --> 00:54:03.820
is going. And then finally there are a lot
of links in the document which link through

00:54:03.820 --> 00:54:08.990
to the national and regional support offer
that we are seeking to make available to systems,

00:54:08.990 --> 00:54:13.700
so that systems will be supported at every
step of the way to create these plans.

00:54:13.700 --> 00:54:19.610
The final thing that I'll just say is that
the framework sets out a timetable and we

00:54:19.610 --> 00:54:25.330
are asking that systems agree their plans
by the middle of November to allow us to bring

00:54:25.330 --> 00:54:32.070
that together into the full national implementation
plan by the end of the year as we have committed

00:54:32.070 --> 00:54:36.890
to do.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thanks very much, Matt. Open

00:54:36.890 --> 00:54:43.120
up for questions, comments. Andrew?
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: A lot of what we're trying

00:54:43.120 --> 00:54:52.230
to do here relies on ICSs and STPs delivering
a different type of service. I'd be interested

00:54:52.230 --> 00:54:56.970
to know to what extent we're going to give,
you know, maximum flexibility to local systems

00:54:56.970 --> 00:55:02.900
to deliver local priorities.
So, for example, in the plan, we've earmarked

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:08.720
many for mental health, cancer, et cetera,
which I assume will get devolved down to a

00:55:08.720 --> 00:55:10.910
system level. To what extent can systems flex
those allocations to meet their own kind of

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:17.010
local needs? So if you're doing really, really
well, for example, on mental health, can you

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:25.580
use that money for cancer, where perhaps you're
doing really, really poorly at a particular

00:55:25.580 --> 00:55:28.890
system?
&gt;&gt;Matthew Tagney: So, within the plan, we've

00:55:28.890 --> 00:55:35.530
set out a particular funding or investment
guarantees around primary committee care and

00:55:35.530 --> 00:55:41.064
mental health, which we will be measuring
and ensuring that we deliver against. Beyond

00:55:41.064 --> 00:55:45.770
that, we are tasking systems to deliver the
outcomes that we have set out in the long-term

00:55:45.770 --> 00:55:47.630
plan within the funding and resources available
to it. So beyond that, there is some flexibility

00:55:47.630 --> 00:55:49.140
to systems, yes.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: Just to say, just to take

00:55:49.140 --> 00:55:57.880
the mental health investments, that we are
absolutely committed each and every year to

00:55:57.880 --> 00:56:02.721
spend the amount of money we have committed
to spend, and indeed are putting a good deal

00:56:02.721 --> 00:56:13.430
of effort into going and auditing and assuring
ourselves that the money is actually being

00:56:13.430 --> 00:56:21.690
spent on the things that it should be spent
on. So I think there are one or two areas

00:56:21.690 --> 00:56:26.000
were absolutely, we are not going to give
the kind of flexibility you describe, and

00:56:26.000 --> 00:56:29.010
I'll say mental health is like number one
on there. And indeed, even the plan in community

00:56:29.010 --> 00:56:33.770
care, again, we had specify the amount of
money we are going to be spending by 2023/2024,

00:56:33.770 --> 00:56:41.080
and we are looking to see people show some
improvements, because we think we will actually

00:56:41.080 --> 00:56:45.821
need to spend that amount of money in order
to deliver the workforce growth we think is

00:56:45.821 --> 00:56:51.030
required and some of the other investments.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Okay. But, I get that, but

00:56:51.030 --> 00:56:56.170
it's where you've got systems in place, say
primary care, I mean this big chunks of money

00:56:56.170 --> 00:57:03.470
set aside there, and in some systems they're
quite well developed and I'm just testing

00:57:03.470 --> 00:57:11.140
whether or not there is some scope to via
money around in [inaudible].

00:57:11.140 --> 00:57:16.950
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Well, I'll just come in behind
what Julian said. The answer is, across the

00:57:16.950 --> 00:57:21.880
board generally yes, but not on mental health
and primary medical and community health services

00:57:21.880 --> 00:57:25.930
because those have been typically the two
parts that have actually got squeezed rather

00:57:25.930 --> 00:57:30.510
than over-invested in, and frankly there is
no part of the country that is currently doing

00:57:30.510 --> 00:57:35.870
so brilliantly on its mental health services
that we can say, okay, take the foot of the

00:57:35.870 --> 00:57:40.970
gas and just at the margin user which would
be mental health resources or something else.

00:57:40.970 --> 00:57:46.130
But that's, you know, that's still a minority
of the total growth that would be available

00:57:46.130 --> 00:57:50.570
locally across the health services where people
have got very significant discretion.

00:57:50.570 --> 00:57:52.980
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Ruth, did you want to come
in?

00:57:52.980 --> 00:57:56.040
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Yes. Thank you. So, thank you,
Matt, and it's really great to see that workforce

00:57:56.040 --> 00:58:03.050
is part of the long-term plan now because
without the workforce we won't be able to

00:58:03.050 --> 00:58:06.671
successfully see the implementation for patients
and for the people that we serve, so thank

00:58:06.671 --> 00:58:11.240
you.
I'm working really closely with Prerana, the

00:58:11.240 --> 00:58:17.700
chief people officer, and indeed colleagues
in all of our ARPs but particularly with HE

00:58:17.700 --> 00:58:23.930
because nursing is a priority within it, and
that parity not just for next year but right

00:58:23.930 --> 00:58:27.700
now. So I just wanted to assure the board
that we're not just looking at this as a plan

00:58:27.700 --> 00:58:34.220
that is going to be sometime in November that
we are going to be working on this. Actually

00:58:34.220 --> 00:58:41.380
right now, we're starting to deliver the interim
people plan. Up to today, we are now up to

00:58:41.380 --> 00:58:47.290
7000 clinical placements being pledged for
this September by providers. So, we've busted

00:58:47.290 --> 00:58:53.100
that 5000 clinical placement target. So I'm
delighted and very grateful to the provider

00:58:53.100 --> 00:59:00.430
sector and to HEI sector for the work they're
doing to really get behind making sure that

00:59:00.430 --> 00:59:09.810
nursing is a reality - parity in the future.
So thank you for that.

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:14.040
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Steve?
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes, thank you, Matt, and I'm

00:59:14.040 --> 00:59:17.970
particularly delighted that in paragraph 5
of the board paper accompanying this, clinically

00:59:17.970 --> 00:59:23.520
led is at the top of the list of requirements
that we are asking our local systems to meet

00:59:23.520 --> 00:59:24.520
in developing their plans. I'm sure you haven't
prioritised it in any particular way, but

00:59:24.520 --> 00:59:27.820
it's great that clinically led is at the start.
And I think that for Ruth and I, but really

00:59:27.820 --> 00:59:35.650
for everybody, I'd like to express the importance
that clinicians get to the heart of the local

00:59:35.650 --> 00:59:42.620
plans, and that means working in the development
both in terms of the content but in terms

00:59:42.620 --> 00:59:48.070
also of the mechanism, particularly in our
integrated care systems, the development of

00:59:48.070 --> 00:59:50.750
integrated care systems, to bringing clinicians
together from primary and secondary care for

00:59:50.750 --> 00:59:56.670
mental health, and also colleagues in local
government to redesign clinical models that

00:59:56.670 --> 01:00:00.610
will deliver the long-term plan.
I know there are many areas of the country

01:00:00.610 --> 01:00:06.180
where those clinical leadership - those clinical
systems are in place and they should absolutely

01:00:06.180 --> 01:00:10.010
be utilised. I think this is also an opportunity
for those areas where that development is

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:15.510
further back to really use this to catalyse
further development of the clinical leadership

01:00:15.510 --> 01:00:20.630
network within the integrated care systems
and STPs.

01:00:20.630 --> 01:00:23.600
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: David. You wanted to come
in?

01:00:23.600 --> 01:00:28.230
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, thanks, Dido. It is more
a comment that the question. I just wanted

01:00:28.230 --> 01:00:33.910
to emphasise this point that I think we drew
out earlier about human capital and the importance

01:00:33.910 --> 01:00:38.430
of investing in that as well. But I think
the plan is welcome and I enjoyed reading

01:00:38.430 --> 01:00:44.160
it. It is very clear. And the work which will
be carried out locally over these next few

01:00:44.160 --> 01:00:50.250
months is hugely important so we can establish
what the demand is in the future for - well,

01:00:50.250 --> 01:00:54.100
present and future, for services. And that's
resource, that's in the 20 billion and the

01:00:54.100 --> 01:00:59.820
investment that is being made. I think the
trick there is to reconcile, and this is where

01:00:59.820 --> 01:01:04.640
the people plan work will come in, to reconcile
that we've got the supply, particularly the

01:01:04.640 --> 01:01:10.560
education and training supply, for the workforce
that can be bought with the 20 billion. And

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:14.780
that's the bit that we need to just ensure
we've got the investment in. At the minute,

01:01:14.780 --> 01:01:19.000
I think we are making assumptions that that
supply is there, rather than we are absolutely

01:01:19.000 --> 01:01:25.370
clear. And I think this is why getting some
clarity about what money is going to move

01:01:25.370 --> 01:01:31.380
where and why is hugely important because
that then, we need to use that as a springboard

01:01:31.380 --> 01:01:35.030
to be clear about what is the workforce that
is required.

01:01:35.030 --> 01:01:40.590
I think the other challenge that we've got
to respond to is this emphasis in the people

01:01:40.590 --> 01:01:45.000
plan about more and different. Ruth has talked
about the importance of being clear about

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:49.910
how many more nurses we need, but we also
need to be clear about what skill mix we want

01:01:49.910 --> 01:01:53.742
to see going through. And I think that's a
further work that needs to be done between

01:01:53.742 --> 01:01:59.680
the autumn. So the planning work which trusts
are going to do between now and the autumn

01:01:59.680 --> 01:02:04.750
needs to come alongside the workforce planning,
particularly at the education planning.

01:02:04.750 --> 01:02:11.940
Work for educational placements in the next
academic year needs to be done like now. So

01:02:11.940 --> 01:02:17.290
just making sure we've got these alignments
is absolutely critical. And then making sure

01:02:17.290 --> 01:02:24.340
that those training places and particularly
placements are in place to ensure the people

01:02:24.340 --> 01:02:29.770
on graduate programmes are coming into those
placements. And I do want to emphasise the

01:02:29.770 --> 01:02:35.260
importance of making sure that every source
plan and not just, this is what we'd like.

01:02:35.260 --> 01:02:41.240
And I think that work still needs to be done.
And whether that's a conversation for the

01:02:41.240 --> 01:02:47.200
SR or that resource will come from other places,
will get played out over the next period of

01:02:47.200 --> 01:02:51.480
time, but it's an absolutely critical element
to the delivery. So, as I say, it's not a

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:57.380
question. I just want to emphasise the importance.
I think what we've managed to do over the

01:02:57.380 --> 01:03:03.451
past few months with the work that you've
led on the interim people plan, Dido, is begin

01:03:03.451 --> 01:03:09.150
to realign service planning, workforce planning,
and financial planning, and having done that,

01:03:09.150 --> 01:03:13.080
we shouldn't let that slip away from us again,
and as a critical piece of work that needs

01:03:13.080 --> 01:03:17.570
to be done in the autumn.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: And it's good to see that

01:03:17.570 --> 01:03:22.460
basic principle embedded in this implementation
plan and framework.

01:03:22.460 --> 01:03:29.480
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Absolutely.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: More clearly than it probably

01:03:29.480 --> 01:03:37.900
has been in recent times.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Dido, you've got that alignment,

01:03:37.900 --> 01:03:45.290
so we now just need to convert that into numbers
and investment proposition.

01:03:45.290 --> 01:03:48.420
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes. At both the local and
the national level. David?

01:03:48.420 --> 01:03:54.050
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And maybe this may be a question
for you, Dido, or for David actually, is that,

01:03:54.050 --> 01:03:58.300
you know, given that it takes 10 years, as
we currently train doctors, it takes 10 years

01:03:58.300 --> 01:04:04.430
for a GP and up to 15 years to be a specialist
in some specialisms, we have no idea of what

01:04:04.430 --> 01:04:08.170
doctors are going to be doing in 10 or 15
years' time or very little idea. I mean, what

01:04:08.170 --> 01:04:15.320
they do will have changed very fundamentally.
So, what conclusions do we draw from that

01:04:15.320 --> 01:04:16.460
for training and how we've trained doctors
now?

01:04:16.460 --> 01:04:18.652
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So, this has been a big part
of the interim people plan, and Steve will

01:04:18.652 --> 01:04:26.150
have a view on this, but you see in the interim
people plan that medical reform programme

01:04:26.150 --> 01:04:31.640
that is outlined in there is really addressing
these issues about how we can create flexibility

01:04:31.640 --> 01:04:37.170
in the way that education, careers are developed,
how we can create flexibility in training

01:04:37.170 --> 01:04:46.060
and education structure to allow our people
to move about. And this is my point about

01:04:46.060 --> 01:04:49.210
- workforce planning is really difficult,
because you are predicting something 15 years

01:04:49.210 --> 01:04:53.920
out when science and technology is moving
quickly. I think the fact this has never been

01:04:53.920 --> 01:04:57.940
landed, in my view, in the history of the
NHS is not because people are incompetent;

01:04:57.940 --> 01:05:03.350
it's because it's really difficult to do.
And I think what we need to do over this next

01:05:03.350 --> 01:05:10.700
period is create some agility in the way that
people are being trained and educated. That

01:05:10.700 --> 01:05:15.850
means an alignment with HEIs, where oncology
is, et cetera, and my sense six months into

01:05:15.850 --> 01:05:22.760
doing the HEI job is that this is a very fragmented
sector with a lack of alignment between some

01:05:22.760 --> 01:05:28.820
of the key parties that need to act in this
sector to advance this debate. But what I

01:05:28.820 --> 01:05:34.250
think has begun to happen over the past six
months is a greater alignment. There was an

01:05:34.250 --> 01:05:38.140
advisory board for the people plan where the
universities, the VCs were represented at

01:05:38.140 --> 01:05:42.370
that. That's the first meeting I'd gone to
where vice chancellors were actually present

01:05:42.370 --> 01:05:47.090
talking about some of the issues around the
future of health education alongside people

01:05:47.090 --> 01:05:51.140
from trusts, the national organisations, et
cetera, et cetera. So I think we are beginning

01:05:51.140 --> 01:05:58.160
to put the conversations in place and beginning
to develop the mechanisms that we all require.

01:05:58.160 --> 01:06:03.960
And this is why this work is important, and
I wanted to make this point, because I absolutely

01:06:03.960 --> 01:06:08.790
support the long-term plans direction of travel,
but as you've just been talking, the trajectory

01:06:08.790 --> 01:06:16.140
is to 2023/2024, and yet the supply line we
need to get for doctors goes well beyond 2023/2024,

01:06:16.140 --> 01:06:21.960
and arguably, we needed to have some clarity
with the universities for the young people

01:06:21.960 --> 01:06:27.420
that are going to start university this autumn,
because they weren't the out and into the

01:06:27.420 --> 01:06:35.661
workforce until at least 2031/2032. What are
they being trained for? What is the world

01:06:35.661 --> 01:06:36.910
that they are being trained for? And I don't
think that we've had enough focus around the

01:06:36.910 --> 01:06:40.660
conversation, to be honest, but that's the
conversation that we've been carrying to the

01:06:40.660 --> 01:06:49.410
VCs and the council of deans, et cetera about
how do we begin to come up with these issues.

01:06:49.410 --> 01:06:53.950
As well as, as Ruth said, the agency of just
making sure that we've got enough nurses and

01:06:53.950 --> 01:06:58.880
nursing associates coming into the system
this year. So it's a hugely important debate,

01:06:58.880 --> 01:07:04.090
and the full people plan is important for
setting not just the period to 2023/2024,

01:07:04.090 --> 01:07:10.070
but beyond 2024 over the next decade or so.
I think the question we need to answer is,

01:07:10.070 --> 01:07:16.140
what will the workforce look like in 10, 15
and 20 years' time? Not just what it will

01:07:16.140 --> 01:07:20.100
look like in 2021, 2022.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Good. Steve.

01:07:20.100 --> 01:07:24.050
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes. So I think that question
was at the heart of the discussions around

01:07:24.050 --> 01:07:28.460
the interim people plan and it's in that chapter
on the 21st century workforce. So, as David

01:07:28.460 --> 01:07:34.470
said. So it's always been the case that the
future is different from the present and it's

01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:40.340
always the case that the next generation will
be working differently, but I think it's going

01:07:40.340 --> 01:07:43.300
to be particularly true of the next 10 to
15 years, for two reasons at the very top

01:07:43.300 --> 01:07:48.410
of my list. One of the digital revolution,
which will, as we will start to hear in a

01:07:48.410 --> 01:07:54.140
few minutes, will increasingly start to change
the way that we work as professionals. I think

01:07:54.140 --> 01:07:58.230
the genetics revolution is another revolution
that is going to occur over the next 10 to

01:07:58.230 --> 01:08:01.720
15 years that will make us think differently
about risk and risk management and population

01:08:01.720 --> 01:08:06.020
management. So, I think for all those reasons,
it's really critical that - and it's not just

01:08:06.020 --> 01:08:10.630
about doctors, but all clinicians think very
hard about how we will be working in the next

01:08:10.630 --> 01:08:19.909
10 to 15 years. And I think that's an international
issue as much as it is a local issue, actually.

01:08:19.909 --> 01:08:25.449
And I think that involves extending roles
and working differently. I think it means

01:08:25.449 --> 01:08:31.029
working in teams much more than we work at
the moment. A whole variety of things. But

01:08:31.029 --> 01:08:35.940
we have to get it the right way round in terms
of training. So I think it's for us as the

01:08:35.940 --> 01:08:43.359
service to describe to the trainers what that
workforce looks like, because then it's the

01:08:43.359 --> 01:08:48.779
task of the universities, the postgraduate
curricula of continued development to allow

01:08:48.779 --> 01:08:54.489
our people, our staff, to develop those skills
and those attributes. So that's a big piece

01:08:54.489 --> 01:08:59.509
of work going forward. Certainly, talking
to the medical Royal colleges who are very

01:08:59.509 --> 01:09:04.699
much part of my stakeholder group on the medical
workforce, they are up for that challenge,

01:09:04.699 --> 01:09:09.380
and I think there's a bit of excitement actually
about thinking quite bravely about what the

01:09:09.380 --> 01:09:11.839
future workforce might look like.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Good.

01:09:11.839 --> 01:09:16.960
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Sorry, can I ask, one of the things
in a more commercial world is you wouldn't

01:09:16.960 --> 01:09:23.449
over specialise too early, and I wonder whether
one of the things we also need to ask ourselves

01:09:23.449 --> 01:09:28.199
is whether it's not just the initial training,
which is the point you are making, but also

01:09:28.199 --> 01:09:35.009
about the pathways by which doctors, nurses,
clinicians, generally develop their careers

01:09:35.009 --> 01:09:42.769
over time. Because I'm struck by the extent
to which specialism happens, and fine specialism,

01:09:42.769 --> 01:09:48.059
happens very early in people's careers, and
is a time where technology is changing the

01:09:48.059 --> 01:09:53.400
way that, and there's a greater integration
between different disciplines, I just wonder

01:09:53.400 --> 01:09:57.099
whether we should also ask ourselves that
question, if how we develop and whether we

01:09:57.099 --> 01:10:00.010
over specialise too soon.
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yes, and that question has

01:10:00.010 --> 01:10:04.949
been asked and the postgraduate curricula
we met are now certainly changing and moving

01:10:04.949 --> 01:10:12.190
much more towards continuing to provide generalist
training up until the point of certification

01:10:12.190 --> 01:10:16.380
as a consultant. But there is a conundrum
in that question, because medicine inevitably

01:10:16.380 --> 01:10:23.139
becomes more specialised, so I often say that
when the NHS was formed in 1948, I think it

01:10:23.139 --> 01:10:28.310
was something around 50 years the medical
knowledge to double, and in 2020, it's estimated

01:10:28.310 --> 01:10:31.599
to be about 75 days for medical knowledge
to double.

01:10:31.599 --> 01:10:37.949
So I think in the workforce of the future,
one of the tensions we need to solve is how

01:10:37.949 --> 01:10:45.610
we provide that specialist depth of knowledge,
with the need to have a generalist underpinning.

01:10:45.610 --> 01:10:50.039
And of course primary care, which again, we're
going to hear about next, as in this country

01:10:50.039 --> 01:10:56.210
always been the bedrock of that generalism.
So, it's a difficult conundrum to solve, but

01:10:56.210 --> 01:11:00.079
we absolutely have to ensure that we solve
it.

01:11:00.079 --> 01:11:01.519
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: All right. David?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think I'm right, Steve, to

01:11:01.519 --> 01:11:05.440
David's point, that's the work that has been
going on about instead of having two years

01:11:05.440 --> 01:11:09.889
of generalism, having three years of generalism
before people specialise in terms of their

01:11:09.889 --> 01:11:15.599
education, to pick when exactly that point,
to stop people specialising too soon in their

01:11:15.599 --> 01:11:19.320
careers, and building that is part of the
education system.

01:11:19.320 --> 01:11:25.690
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any other comments on the
implementation and planning framework? In

01:11:25.690 --> 01:11:30.510
which case, I think we should congratulate
you, Matt, and the team who worked on this.

01:11:30.510 --> 01:11:36.559
I know that actually there's been a huge effort
to consult and engage and shape it in a way

01:11:36.559 --> 01:11:41.950
that will generally help systems to build
plans that will make a huge difference, so

01:11:41.950 --> 01:11:49.469
well done. Thank you very much. Good.
Game of musical chairs. So, we now move on

01:11:49.469 --> 01:11:55.530
to primary care. We should have Ed Waller,
Dominic Hardy and Nikki Kanani, you're going

01:11:55.530 --> 01:12:03.050
to sit on two seats, I think. There you go.
Fantastic. Ian, are you going to kick us off?

01:12:03.050 --> 01:12:09.710
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Great. Thank you very much. This
paper covers a lot of important content across

01:12:09.710 --> 01:12:16.080
seven different topics. First, the new funding
guarantee for primary medical and community

01:12:16.080 --> 01:12:24.380
health services. It comes into effect next
April. A minimum cash floor in every region

01:12:24.380 --> 01:12:30.989
against a baseline of 2018/2019 plan spend,
and as Julian has already said, a minimum

01:12:30.989 --> 01:12:38.400
cash floor in every ICS by 2023/2024. And
this guarantee is of immense interest to community

01:12:38.400 --> 01:12:45.639
providers and primary care, as well as CCGs
and STPs. It's prominent in the long-term

01:12:45.639 --> 01:12:50.599
plan implementation framework, and I encourage
all to clock now what it means.

01:12:50.599 --> 01:12:58.790
Second session. The NHS now has its inaugural
director of community health services. By

01:12:58.790 --> 01:13:04.250
autumn, Matthew Wynne will have staffed up
his new group working alongside the team here

01:13:04.250 --> 01:13:13.550
with clear priorities. The key to our two
day targets the crisis care and re-enablement,

01:13:13.550 --> 01:13:19.900
anticipatory care for complex patients and
care home support, both jointly with primary

01:13:19.900 --> 01:13:27.179
care, set out in the national service specifications
for community services for the first time.

01:13:27.179 --> 01:13:32.770
Phased in line with the funding guarantee
of investment.

01:13:32.770 --> 01:13:41.119
But these goals need considerable workforce
expansion and workforce productivity gains,

01:13:41.119 --> 01:13:48.050
so we'll be working with Prerana, with help
from Hugh implementing Pat, your report on

01:13:48.050 --> 01:13:54.329
efficiency in community services, and with
help from Matthew Gould on upping our work

01:13:54.329 --> 01:14:02.639
on digital in community services.
Third section. The new five-year GP contract

01:14:02.639 --> 01:14:09.820
marks the biggest changes since 2004. It's
landed pretty well. Major changes have already

01:14:09.820 --> 01:14:17.079
been implemented and are being felt, such
as indemnity and reformed GP quality and outcomes

01:14:17.079 --> 01:14:24.139
framework. And I'd like to highlight three
additional specifics today. So first, we can

01:14:24.139 --> 01:14:32.510
confirm the pipeline of seven further quality
improvement modules for the QOF, and mindful

01:14:32.510 --> 01:14:37.150
of Matthews and Joanne's earlier comments
about the importance of learning disability,

01:14:37.150 --> 01:14:45.179
I'm particularly delighted to announce agreement
with the BMA to seek early delivery of our

01:14:45.179 --> 01:14:52.010
learning disability commitments in primary
care. So, a focus on improved practice registers,

01:14:52.010 --> 01:14:59.400
more flu jabs, given high respiratory mortality
are shown in the leader reports, and the ambition

01:14:59.400 --> 01:15:06.869
for early delivery of the 75% coverage goal
in the long-term plan for comprehensive learning

01:15:06.869 --> 01:15:13.460
disability health checks, aided by a new QOF
quality improvement module for learning disability

01:15:13.460 --> 01:15:20.409
coming in 2020/2021 and a national campaign.
And the third thing also pick up in this section

01:15:20.409 --> 01:15:26.929
relates to the conversation we've just been
having around workforce. So, David, you talked

01:15:26.929 --> 01:15:32.739
about the importance of skill mix. We have
a plan for 20,000 additional staff in very

01:15:32.739 --> 01:15:37.530
specific roles where we know we have both
demand and supply, but we also know we need

01:15:37.530 --> 01:15:43.699
to do more on GP and indeed the nurse numbers.
And today's paper sets out some of the additional

01:15:43.699 --> 01:15:49.599
approaches we are exploring for the final
people plan, how to make it more attractive

01:15:49.599 --> 01:15:56.429
for sessional GPs to increase their time commitments
to primary care networks, a clearer national

01:15:56.429 --> 01:16:03.440
guide path from postgraduate training through
into working in general practice, and every

01:16:03.440 --> 01:16:09.249
examination with a cheap colleagues of our
national modelling assumptions within the

01:16:09.249 --> 01:16:15.280
context of the spending review. Again, working
very closely with Prerana.

01:16:15.280 --> 01:16:20.760
The fourth section covers the formation of
primary care networks before next Mondays

01:16:20.760 --> 01:16:28.730
go live date. In this highly complex exercise
has stimulated what's ended up looking like

01:16:28.730 --> 01:16:38.420
1259 legally binding alliances between practices
without gaps across England, in line with

01:16:38.420 --> 01:16:48.130
our rules. With only 26 of nearly 7000 practices
opting out, only a handful expecting to join

01:16:48.130 --> 01:16:56.869
and wanting to but not likely to be in a network
by the 1st of July, and only 26 networks that

01:16:56.869 --> 01:17:04.659
are smaller than 27,000 populations due to
rurality. So this we think is 99.7% coverage

01:17:04.659 --> 01:17:13.280
of practices. The numbers have improved in
just the last few minutes since Matthew revealed

01:17:13.280 --> 01:17:16.880
that 98%.
These numbers are unconfirmed reports from

01:17:16.880 --> 01:17:23.749
CCGs, and the position is changing daily,
so the final numbers for the 1st of July will

01:17:23.749 --> 01:17:28.659
probably be slightly different. And there
are some notable teething problems. This is

01:17:28.659 --> 01:17:34.690
inevitable, and Ed's been grappling with a
number of the hardest of those. What's really

01:17:34.690 --> 01:17:41.079
striking as we are seeing a new generation
of enthusiastic primary care leaders coming

01:17:41.079 --> 01:17:46.900
to the fore as clinical directors. I was really
struck by this. We had a great joint event

01:17:46.900 --> 01:17:53.090
with the BMA recently and the BMA team and
our team, we didn't know at least half the

01:17:53.090 --> 01:17:59.650
people in the audience, and the event was
massively oversubscribed. I mean, I thought

01:17:59.650 --> 01:18:05.079
that this process might go better than I was
expecting, but not by this much.

01:18:05.079 --> 01:18:17.989
&gt;&gt;Speaker: That's such a meta point.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I was just checking who was listening.

01:18:17.989 --> 01:18:23.809
General practice has stepped up, and I'd also
like to thank the huge amount of work undertaken

01:18:23.809 --> 01:18:29.489
by CCGs working really well with the local
medical committees, the local branches of

01:18:29.489 --> 01:18:35.190
the BMA, and our regions.
The fifth section, the formation of primary

01:18:35.190 --> 01:18:40.280
care networks, is of course just the start.
We have a significant development and support

01:18:40.280 --> 01:18:47.460
plan, and just creating a network does not
overnight solve the considerable workload

01:18:47.460 --> 01:18:52.420
and workforce challenges in general practice.
We need to continually remember this. But

01:18:52.420 --> 01:18:59.639
what will success look like if we look forward
a number of years? First of all, they will

01:18:59.639 --> 01:19:04.909
have stabilised general practice, and we've
given the existing partnership model a major

01:19:04.909 --> 01:19:11.550
shot in the arm through the new contract.
It will now be down to PCMs to ensure that

01:19:11.550 --> 01:19:16.630
there is a new generation of partners if the
independent contractor model is to endure

01:19:16.630 --> 01:19:21.320
in 2030.
Primary care networks will have helped tackle

01:19:21.320 --> 01:19:26.480
some of the capacity gaps. They will have
employed over 20,000 extra pharmacists, physios,

01:19:26.480 --> 01:19:31.010
paramedics, physician associates and social
prescribers, who become an integral part of

01:19:31.010 --> 01:19:37.800
the practice team. Networks will be moving
from the current, more introverted process

01:19:37.800 --> 01:19:43.590
of practices joining together to looking out,
and above all this is the critical point of

01:19:43.590 --> 01:19:49.699
having done those things, PCM is will have
been able to have shown quantified impacts

01:19:49.699 --> 01:19:54.920
for their patients in the wider NHS, thus
justifying the funding guarantee. That is

01:19:54.920 --> 01:19:59.749
the purpose of setting these up, and that
is what we are looking for over the next five

01:19:59.749 --> 01:20:04.900
years.
The sixth section of the paper covers digital

01:20:04.900 --> 01:20:12.050
primary care, and the board's asked to approve
the launch of a new consultation. This honours

01:20:12.050 --> 01:20:18.139
the commitment to review the out of area registration
rules and associated payment arrangements,

01:20:18.139 --> 01:20:24.820
and we are seeking views by the 22nd of August.
Under the terms of the new contract, all patients

01:20:24.820 --> 01:20:32.300
will have a right to digital-first primary
care by 2021 from their existing practices,

01:20:32.300 --> 01:20:40.510
and our most important task is to help existing
practices digitise, supported by a new supplier

01:20:40.510 --> 01:20:44.579
framework.
We've also seen the emergence of digital-first

01:20:44.579 --> 01:20:51.469
practices directly registering patients, in
competition with existing GPs, using the current

01:20:51.469 --> 01:20:57.659
out of area rules. And this means, for example,
that the GP at hand practice in Hammersmith

01:20:57.659 --> 01:21:05.039
has a wide distribution of patients all across
London, which cuts across the need to integrate

01:21:05.039 --> 01:21:11.789
care in local networks, as well as causing
funding issues for Hammersmith CCG. And we

01:21:11.789 --> 01:21:19.889
are proposing to fix this issue by creating
in regulations a new mechanism to disaggregate

01:21:19.889 --> 01:21:26.820
the practice list automatically when a threshold
number of out of our patients is reached in

01:21:26.820 --> 01:21:33.570
any CCG, and we are suggesting that's between
1000 and 2000, and this would trigger the

01:21:33.570 --> 01:21:39.210
automatic creation of a new practice list
for GP at hand in, say, Tower Hamlets, where

01:21:39.210 --> 01:21:45.430
they would need to provide premises, be part
of a local Tower Hamlets network, and meet

01:21:45.430 --> 01:21:50.550
all service requirements.
We also propose a new quarterly adjustment

01:21:50.550 --> 01:21:56.579
to CCG allocations, working closely with Julian
and his team, to reflect better the movement

01:21:56.579 --> 01:22:03.940
of patients while solving the Hammersmith
CCG funding problem. The consultation also

01:22:03.940 --> 01:22:08.099
analyses and makes recommendations on changing
specific payment rules.

01:22:08.099 --> 01:22:14.829
And finally, it starts an important conversation
about whether new national rules on establishing

01:22:14.829 --> 01:22:20.999
new practices in our most under doctored areas
could help tackle the inverse care law and

01:22:20.999 --> 01:22:26.130
inequalities.
Seventh and finally, we publish our interim

01:22:26.130 --> 01:22:32.559
review of GP premises. We'll be taking forward
a number of recommendations. The biggest issue,

01:22:32.559 --> 01:22:39.849
and this relates I think, Dido, to your comment
about the capital challenges we face, is the

01:22:39.849 --> 01:22:45.699
scale of premises that are not fit for purpose.
And making progress on that clearly depends

01:22:45.699 --> 01:22:49.150
on the capital settlement in the spending
review.

01:22:49.150 --> 01:22:55.060
To help with what is known as the last man
standing problem, we are committing the NHS

01:22:55.060 --> 01:23:01.099
to standing behind some leases. Exactly which
ones to be worked through and discussed including

01:23:01.099 --> 01:23:07.329
with the BMA. Standing behind some leases
of strategic importance. We'll be piloting

01:23:07.329 --> 01:23:13.130
new types of premises reimbursement at network
level, and will be encouraging primary care

01:23:13.130 --> 01:23:18.570
networks to think about premises needs at
network level rather than just individual

01:23:18.570 --> 01:23:25.869
practice level and jointly with their community
partners. I recommend the paper to the board

01:23:25.869 --> 01:23:31.210
and specifically seek endorsement to launch
the consultation on digital first.

01:23:31.210 --> 01:23:41.389
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Fantastic. Thank you. Noel?
&gt;&gt;Noel: Yeah. Can I just offer a comment in

01:23:41.389 --> 01:23:47.949
support particularly of Ian's initiative around
chapter 4 of the consultation around digital-first

01:23:47.949 --> 01:23:56.929
in primary care to support our inequalities
programme? I think it's bold, it's brave,

01:23:56.929 --> 01:24:04.239
it's absolutely the right thing to do. But
I would also suggest in the consultation process

01:24:04.239 --> 01:24:09.800
that the evaluation mechanism to understand
whether it's making an impact or not really

01:24:09.800 --> 01:24:16.360
picks up on the learnings that we've had over
the last year in introducing the NHS act into

01:24:16.360 --> 01:24:21.229
a variety of areas where, as you know, we
test and test and test again against the whole

01:24:21.229 --> 01:24:27.219
diversity of the population. Some things work,
and the engagement was also very good, some

01:24:27.219 --> 01:24:32.060
things don't work quite so well. And I think
it's really key to broaden out what we mean

01:24:32.060 --> 01:24:39.369
by digital first in supporting the inequalities
programme. Sometimes we found text actually,

01:24:39.369 --> 01:24:45.599
bizarrely, is far more effective than more
sophisticated tools. So there's a breadth

01:24:45.599 --> 01:24:52.550
of what we mean by digital-first to support
inequalities that I'd encourage the evaluation

01:24:52.550 --> 01:24:57.693
process to test a range of possible digital
tools and not just go for the, let's say,

01:24:57.693 --> 01:25:03.979
the most obvious, which is through an app,
because we are still learning a huge amount

01:25:03.979 --> 01:25:09.789
here, and there is a lot that we do not know
about what really is effective in primary

01:25:09.789 --> 01:25:18.729
care and digital first and what is less effective.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: So, a great point, and we'll take

01:25:18.729 --> 01:25:23.280
that away and taking into account exactly
as you suggest, really helpful.

01:25:23.280 --> 01:25:31.070
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Other comments. Pat?
&gt;&gt;Pat: Well, I'll just say how welcome this

01:25:31.070 --> 01:25:39.510
is and particularly the solutions to solving
some of the challenges we face in GP land.

01:25:39.510 --> 01:25:46.840
And to really take the lead on this internationally,
it seems to me. I've looked to Tim Ferris

01:25:46.840 --> 01:25:50.840
there, but I think if we can get this out
there and working, it will be really significant

01:25:50.840 --> 01:25:55.989
for our population, and particularly in those
deprived areas, and it will also show other

01:25:55.989 --> 01:26:02.780
countries what we can actually do. I think
it's really important for our standing that

01:26:02.780 --> 01:26:09.560
we do this quickly, wholeheartedly, and undoubtedly
it will raise issues with colleagues, et cetera,

01:26:09.560 --> 01:26:14.809
but we have to be resolute in pushing on and
demonstrating we can actually deliver it.

01:26:14.809 --> 01:26:17.840
But I'm really delighted to see this work.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Joanne, would you?

01:26:17.840 --> 01:26:23.170
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thank you. I think there's
a lot in here and it's very exciting and challenging

01:26:23.170 --> 01:26:26.380
also. I wondered if you might tell as a little
bit more about how you think the digital-first

01:26:26.380 --> 01:26:31.219
initiatives might help with some of the GP
shortage is that we have, and opportunities

01:26:31.219 --> 01:26:40.780
it might offer to GPs? Maybe that's a Nikki
question, actually. So, the interaction between

01:26:40.780 --> 01:26:43.719
digital and the workforce.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Nikki?

01:26:43.719 --> 01:26:49.710
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: Great question. Thank you,
Joanne. One of the things that we know is

01:26:49.710 --> 01:26:54.920
that we've got the severest GP shortages in
areas of socio-economic deprivation. The other

01:26:54.920 --> 01:26:58.729
thing we know is that our workforce is changing
and wants to work differently. So, I went

01:26:58.729 --> 01:27:04.349
to Frimley recently and I met a salary GP
who spends one day a week clinically in practice,

01:27:04.349 --> 01:27:10.229
two days being a mom, the fourth day, she
is a GP, but she does it from home. So she

01:27:10.229 --> 01:27:14.190
does the drop off, does telephone triage,
does all her admin, picks up, puts the kids

01:27:14.190 --> 01:27:17.659
to bed, and then finishes doing her telephone
triage, because that works for the population

01:27:17.659 --> 01:27:22.369
as well. They want to get their calls after
work. What we need to be able to do is create

01:27:22.369 --> 01:27:26.210
a system where our workforce can work more
flexibly and joined up and be at home and

01:27:26.210 --> 01:27:30.360
be able to access a record and do the referrals
and everything else, but also create the capacity

01:27:30.360 --> 01:27:34.139
in places where people don't want to work
at the moment for lots of reasons, but it

01:27:34.139 --> 01:27:39.269
does tend to be areas that face more socio-economic
deprivation. So, if we can create a space

01:27:39.269 --> 01:27:44.960
where we can build the capacity, even if it's
virtual, then it gives a chance to those populations.

01:27:44.960 --> 01:27:50.860
On Noel's point about the app and texting,
we have right back texting in our GP system,

01:27:50.860 --> 01:27:55.619
and, you know, you will get certain members
of the population who will only ever text

01:27:55.619 --> 01:27:59.289
back through that tell us what's going on,
because it's just too overwhelming to get

01:27:59.289 --> 01:28:03.570
the practice. So, I think we've got two really
clear ways through this consultation to work

01:28:03.570 --> 01:28:08.610
on a better offer for the population but also
to offer a more flexible approach for our

01:28:08.610 --> 01:28:10.880
workforce.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: And do you think, Nikki, that

01:28:10.880 --> 01:28:13.739
that might bring more doctor capacity back
into work?

01:28:13.739 --> 01:28:18.489
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: Yeah. I mean, one of the things
that, in fact one of the challenges that Simon

01:28:18.489 --> 01:28:22.309
set me very early on, was actually, if we
could get everyone working just one more session

01:28:22.309 --> 01:28:28.830
each, then we probably go a long way towards
tackling our recruitment and retention issues.

01:28:28.830 --> 01:28:32.699
And this is about bringing back some of the
joy and value at work the general practice

01:28:32.699 --> 01:28:36.309
feels that it's lost. And it actually if you
can work in a more flexible way, and if you

01:28:36.309 --> 01:28:40.329
can spread the workload with the people that
we are bringing in through the contract, then

01:28:40.329 --> 01:28:44.369
actually people will come back and work more.
And the other thing that will bring back to

01:28:44.369 --> 01:28:46.550
local places our sessional workforce. So at
the moment, our sessional workforce is fairly

01:28:46.550 --> 01:28:52.110
scattered. And I've just spent a huge amount
of time with them and said, what is it that

01:28:52.110 --> 01:28:55.789
you miss? They want education and training,
but actually, they want to work within a smaller

01:28:55.789 --> 01:28:59.389
geography. And if they can technologically
be supported to do that, we'll get the participation,

01:28:59.389 --> 01:29:03.710
and we'll make sure that we can build continuity
back into the system.

01:29:03.710 --> 01:29:04.800
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Great. That sounds very good.
Thank you.

01:29:04.800 --> 01:29:05.800
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Tim, do you want a turn?
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: Just to pick up on Patrick's

01:29:05.800 --> 01:29:14.860
comment and reinforce it and reinforce all
the positive comments around here. This is,

01:29:14.860 --> 01:29:25.840
I would say reviewing the paper and the thoughtfulness
and the beta testing that you've done, and

01:29:25.840 --> 01:29:31.960
the early launchers, I think there is no doubt
in my mind that this programme, for all the

01:29:31.960 --> 01:29:36.829
challenges that you face, for the unintended
things that will occur in the future, it is

01:29:36.829 --> 01:29:45.699
nonetheless - it shows extraordinary promise,
and I would say very directly international

01:29:45.699 --> 01:29:52.750
leadership. This will lead the head of what
many other countries, including my own, have

01:29:52.750 --> 01:30:01.809
been working so hard to achieve and yet have
only had spotty uptake. So, I'm very encouraged

01:30:01.809 --> 01:30:11.639
and I look forward to hearing the next six,
nine, 12-month reports, because I think they

01:30:11.639 --> 01:30:14.070
are going to be very positive.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you, Tim. David, you

01:30:14.070 --> 01:30:16.420
wanted to come in?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yes. It's a question I think

01:30:16.420 --> 01:30:22.469
the Nikki, really, that when Ian was doing
his introduction, he said that this was a

01:30:22.469 --> 01:30:29.880
real sort of boost for sort of the partnership
model of GP care. Now, clearly this could

01:30:29.880 --> 01:30:37.489
go one of two ways are in both ways. But what
was - if you were making a well-informed guess

01:30:37.489 --> 01:30:43.769
of what primary care might look like in 10
years, or what GPs might look like in 10 years'

01:30:43.769 --> 01:30:48.630
time, do you think the partnership model is
going to be as strong in 10 years' time as

01:30:48.630 --> 01:30:51.380
it is now?
&gt;&gt;Nikki Kanani: Yeah, I think it - I'm sure

01:30:51.380 --> 01:30:56.570
you've tested this question on me on a visit,
David. So I think it will be stronger, actually.

01:30:56.570 --> 01:31:01.050
If we get this right, and I believe we will,
I think it will be stronger. What's happened

01:31:01.050 --> 01:31:08.139
to the partnership model is that because of
the pressure for the workload, for the workforce,

01:31:08.139 --> 01:31:12.610
and we rehearsed the reasons, people have
stopped finding it attractive and necessarily

01:31:12.610 --> 01:31:15.519
encouraging others to come into it, and it
sort of this associated from the true values

01:31:15.519 --> 01:31:23.030
of kind of traditional general practice. So
I think we can uphold those values and strengthen

01:31:23.030 --> 01:31:28.091
it and encourage people to come back into
partnership. What I do think as we will still

01:31:28.091 --> 01:31:32.239
have a workforce that floats at the network
level, because I don't think we can get away

01:31:32.239 --> 01:31:37.119
from the fact that our workforce does want
to work differently, in a more portfolio would

01:31:37.119 --> 01:31:41.579
way, in a more flexible way, that we've picked
up through the work of the people plan. So,

01:31:41.579 --> 01:31:46.269
we won't get away from that entirely, but
I think the partnership model will feel slightly

01:31:46.269 --> 01:31:51.800
different. It will be okay to be a six-session
partner and to do a day a week at home with

01:31:51.800 --> 01:31:56.139
your digital consultations. We will change
the way that we describe partnership so it

01:31:56.139 --> 01:32:00.099
isn't just about owning premises, but it is
about rebuilding that relationship with your

01:32:00.099 --> 01:32:04.640
patient and that family. And I think we'll
bring more people back into partnership, and

01:32:04.640 --> 01:32:06.280
we'll have a mixed - a blended workforce model
that reflects the population as well. It won't

01:32:06.280 --> 01:32:10.269
be the same in every part of the country,
and that's the beauty of the network model.

01:32:10.269 --> 01:32:18.010
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Can I add, David, I think at the
moment in the last few years, there's been

01:32:18.010 --> 01:32:24.670
a sense of decline, and the fear of potentially
terminal decline of the partnership model.

01:32:24.670 --> 01:32:29.230
That's been the concern of GPs. It's been
harder and harder to get new GP partners.

01:32:29.230 --> 01:32:34.880
And what this is doing, and before that, the
work on the GP forward view is, stabilise

01:32:34.880 --> 01:32:40.059
the situation and make sure that actually
it's a choice for the profession. So, in some

01:32:40.059 --> 01:32:46.030
places, some GPs will want to work more closely,
possibly directly with and for their acute

01:32:46.030 --> 01:32:51.289
and community partners. And that's fine, if
that's the choice they want to make. We are

01:32:51.289 --> 01:32:55.860
getting to the position where actually by
stabilising it, it will be up to the profession

01:32:55.860 --> 01:32:57.530
itself to decide which of those two paths
they want.

01:32:57.530 --> 01:32:59.269
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Fantastic. Any further comments?
Congratulations on a very good piece of work.

01:32:59.269 --> 01:33:08.510
Well done.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Well, thanks to the teams and

01:33:08.510 --> 01:33:22.519
the many others that have done the work.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Absolutely. Well done. So,

01:33:22.519 --> 01:33:29.889
moving on. So, we are now moving onto items
which shouldn't routinely be prescribed in

01:33:29.889 --> 01:33:31.769
primary care. Steve, are you talking to this?
We should have Graham. Hello, Graham.

01:33:31.769 --> 01:33:33.690
&gt;&gt;Graham Jackson: Hello.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Graham Jackson has joined

01:33:33.690 --> 01:33:35.389
us.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Yes, thank you, Dido, and it's

01:33:35.389 --> 01:33:42.039
a pleasure to welcome Dr Graham Jackson, who
is the co-chair of the clinical working group

01:33:42.039 --> 01:33:47.349
that underpins this work, to join us at the
board table and to provide the richness of

01:33:47.349 --> 01:33:52.670
answers to the questions that I reply with
richness, and Alex Williams from our policy

01:33:52.670 --> 01:34:00.360
teams will provide even more richness where
Graham needs that as well. So as a team here

01:34:00.360 --> 01:34:03.420
- [cross-talk]
&gt;&gt;Speaker: So, as the board will remember,

01:34:03.420 --> 01:34:11.590
about 18 months ago in November 2017, we published
guidance to CCGs from NHS England jointly

01:34:11.590 --> 01:34:16.989
with NHS clinical commissioners on a list
of items you should not routinely prescribe

01:34:16.989 --> 01:34:23.099
in primary care, and that included recommendations
on 18 items. And we committed to reviewing

01:34:23.099 --> 01:34:27.809
the guidance at least annually, and that has
been done, and the clinical working group

01:34:27.809 --> 01:34:35.050
undertook a review during the summer of 2018.
And of those recommendations that were made

01:34:35.050 --> 01:34:40.849
previously, that were proposed there should
be an update one of them which was the use

01:34:40.849 --> 01:34:43.769
of capsaicin cream and that is in the updated
guidance.

01:34:43.769 --> 01:34:48.249
But the clinical working group also proposed
an additional eight items be included in the

01:34:48.249 --> 01:34:56.679
CCG guidance that would then be consulted
upon. So those were five of relatively low

01:34:56.679 --> 01:35:01.900
clinical effectiveness, or which are unsafe,
and that includes amiodarone and dronedarone

01:35:01.900 --> 01:35:08.900
which are drugs used to treat abnormal rhythms
of the heart; bath and shower preparations

01:35:08.900 --> 01:35:13.860
used in dry and pruritic skin conditions,
minocycline which is a treatment for acne,

01:35:13.860 --> 01:35:19.099
and silk garments which are predominantly
used in the treatment of eczema. And then

01:35:19.099 --> 01:35:24.130
three items where we know that these items
are clinically effective but where there are

01:35:24.130 --> 01:35:29.860
more cost-effective items available, so that's
[inaudible], which is a treatment for blood

01:35:29.860 --> 01:35:36.360
pressure, and in diabetes, blood glucose testing
strips for type two diabetes and needs for

01:35:36.360 --> 01:35:41.699
prefilled and reusable insulin pens.
So, a three-month formal public consultation

01:35:41.699 --> 01:35:48.039
on the new draft CCG guidance was undertaken
between the 28th of November last year on

01:35:48.039 --> 01:35:52.580
the 28th of February this year, and the results
of that consultation are presented in the

01:35:52.580 --> 01:35:58.800
board papers that you have in front of you
today, along with the updated guidance as

01:35:58.800 --> 01:36:03.880
a result. The guidance highlights some changes
that were made to the draft proposals as a

01:36:03.880 --> 01:36:09.170
result of the consultation. So, the definition
of 'routinely' was slightly amended to reflect

01:36:09.170 --> 01:36:13.909
that the recommendations apply to the routine
prescribing of items in primary care and not

01:36:13.909 --> 01:36:19.280
to patients where there is a clinically exceptional
reason for a prescription. There was some

01:36:19.280 --> 01:36:28.210
updated links, shared care guidance for amiodarone
and dronedarone, and in particular for needles

01:36:28.210 --> 01:36:32.400
for prefilled and reusable insulin pens, clarification
on safety.

01:36:32.400 --> 01:36:39.429
And there was one item that we have removed
for the moment from the guidance, and that

01:36:39.429 --> 01:36:46.750
is on blood glucose testing strips, because
as a result of information that was presented

01:36:46.750 --> 01:36:52.170
to us in the consultation and further information
from our diabetes team in NHS England and

01:36:52.170 --> 01:36:57.010
NHS improvement, we want to do a bit more
work on understanding the differences - the

01:36:57.010 --> 01:37:01.749
potential differences in quality of strips,
and also the relationship between the strips

01:37:01.749 --> 01:37:07.749
and the machines that are used to put the
strips in to get the results. We believe that

01:37:07.749 --> 01:37:13.849
we need more detailed work on that, which
we will undertake before making a recommendation.

01:37:13.849 --> 01:37:20.269
And then finally on the bath and shower preparations
and for the dry and pruritic skin conditions

01:37:20.269 --> 01:37:26.860
and silk garments, we also undertook some
additional evidence reviews by our specialist

01:37:26.860 --> 01:37:32.199
pharmacy service, and those evidence reviews
are also provided for you in the pack.

01:37:32.199 --> 01:37:38.440
So, that has resulted in the guidance that
you have before us today, and so I'd formally

01:37:38.440 --> 01:37:43.020
like the board to consider and note the findings
of the public consultation in relation to

01:37:43.020 --> 01:37:48.289
that one updated item and the eight new items,
and to approve the final recommendations and

01:37:48.289 --> 01:37:51.719
the publication and dissemination of updated
guidance to CCGs.

01:37:51.719 --> 01:37:56.979
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I should just say, this is
an item for the NHS England board, but one

01:37:56.979 --> 01:38:04.840
we were comfortable bringing to the joint
boards in common. Any comments? Questions?

01:38:04.840 --> 01:38:07.989
Munir?
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: I am a clinician, and

01:38:07.989 --> 01:38:15.949
I just want to welcome these gestures. I just
wanted to clarify whether amiodarone and dronedarone,

01:38:15.949 --> 01:38:21.959
are those to stop at initiation, or for repeat
prescription as well? Does it cover both?

01:38:21.959 --> 01:38:25.690
Is it just going to be special prescriptions
for some of those drugs?

01:38:25.690 --> 01:38:28.860
&gt;&gt;Speaker: That's a good question. Those drugs
have, as you're aware, have had a decline

01:38:28.860 --> 01:38:35.039
in their usage over the last few years due
to really the safety concerns and other alternatives.

01:38:35.039 --> 01:38:39.530
So I think we are advising not to start new
patients on them, but absolutely, we'd be

01:38:39.530 --> 01:38:42.010
struck to de-prescribing where appropriate.
So it's very important in these guidelines

01:38:42.010 --> 01:38:47.619
that we don't set the hare running and get
people taken off drugs and destabilised.

01:38:47.619 --> 01:38:53.170
There are people who are stable on those drugs
for many, many years, and are clinically appropriately

01:38:53.170 --> 01:38:58.459
treated. So, individual cases need to be reviewed.
If there is a case for review and may be consideration

01:38:58.459 --> 01:39:02.849
with consultant colleagues from primary care
to look at alternatives, then we would advise

01:39:02.849 --> 01:39:07.130
that, but certainly it's more an issue of
not initiating those, but doing their clinical

01:39:07.130 --> 01:39:09.619
structure of de-prescribing as clinically
appropriate.

01:39:09.619 --> 01:39:13.479
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Joanne?
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Steve, Graham, thank you very

01:39:13.479 --> 01:39:20.070
much. I mean, I think this stuff is very difficult
to do, and I think that it's only achievable

01:39:20.070 --> 01:39:23.230
because you go about it in such a sort of
rigorous and consultative way, so there's

01:39:23.230 --> 01:39:26.989
a massive amount of work in this. I wondered
if you might like to say little bit more about

01:39:26.989 --> 01:39:33.610
how we go from here, the guidance, into implementation,
and how to make sure that this guidance is

01:39:33.610 --> 01:39:38.380
followed in the future. How does that work?
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Graham, do you want to start?

01:39:38.380 --> 01:39:42.550
&gt;&gt;Graham Jackson: Well, I can certainly start.
So its very important throughout the board

01:39:42.550 --> 01:39:48.559
meetings that we remind ourselves that we
are creating commissioning guidance. So, guidance

01:39:48.559 --> 01:39:53.750
for CCGs to implement. And clearly as NHS
seek, CCGs are the ones who have the legal

01:39:53.750 --> 01:40:01.289
responsibility to do that. And we have to
pull ourselves back sometimes in saying, we

01:40:01.289 --> 01:40:05.339
are not creating clinical guidance. That is
created by very erudite other areas. So, the

01:40:05.339 --> 01:40:08.250
best we can do is with the best evidence to
create the best commissioning guidance to

01:40:08.250 --> 01:40:13.679
the CCGs, but then there is the issue about
monitoring and ongoing feedback from CCG.

01:40:13.679 --> 01:40:17.679
Now, we do that through NHS clinical commissioners,
supporting our members, but there are also

01:40:17.679 --> 01:40:21.429
other ways looking at prescribing reviews
and looking at prescribing data. So, I think

01:40:21.429 --> 01:40:26.229
that we have seen, I mean, you've seen some
data here, we have seen a fall off already

01:40:26.229 --> 01:40:30.469
in prescribing. We have done a lot of data
sharing between CCGs, peer review, we've got

01:40:30.469 --> 01:40:35.809
some great graphs that show variation beyond
demographic variation. So we know that this

01:40:35.809 --> 01:40:41.219
stuff can work appropriately for the outcomes
of our patients. So, it is commissioning guidance.

01:40:41.219 --> 01:40:43.382
We need to make sure the CCGs are bought into
that.

01:40:43.382 --> 01:40:44.382
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Yeah, so of course, this is clinically-based
guidance and the principles of all these programmes

01:40:44.382 --> 01:40:53.190
is that it's based on clinical guidance and
a clinical evidence base, but of course we

01:40:53.190 --> 01:40:59.920
can track in a number of ways, including financially,
because we can translate an anticipated change

01:40:59.920 --> 01:41:08.219
in activity into a change in cost. And I can
say that in 1920 our tracking is broadly on

01:41:08.219 --> 01:41:17.270
track at the moment as against our expectations.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any other comments? Excellent.

01:41:17.270 --> 01:41:24.739
In which case, is the NHS England board happy
to approve? Fantastic.

01:41:24.739 --> 01:41:32.999
[cross-talk]
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you very much, Graham.

01:41:32.999 --> 01:41:44.659
So, Jessica, next item, standing out in just
a report from our board committees in common.

01:41:44.659 --> 01:42:01.840
&gt;&gt;Jessica Dahlstrom: Yes. There's nothing
particular to draw to your attention on this,

01:42:01.840 --> 01:42:17.429
unless the chairs of the committees want to
make any comments.

01:42:17.429 --> 01:42:30.199
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Richard and Joanne, is there
anything you want me to raise there?

01:42:30.199 --> 01:42:47.710
&gt;&gt;Richard: I don't think there's anything.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: We don't think there is anything.

01:42:47.710 --> 01:42:52.590
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Good.
&gt;&gt;Richard: Given the angle, report time, and

01:42:52.590 --> 01:42:58.341
the counts, I don't know whether Julian perhaps
wants to sort of do a quick update for the

01:42:58.341 --> 01:43:01.659
whole board, but then this would be signed
off through the committee on where the accounting

01:43:01.659 --> 01:43:10.119
and the final report process is at?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So, I think it is true that

01:43:10.119 --> 01:43:16.030
both NHS I and NHS E that the accounts are
now finalised. Certainly, we had been waiting

01:43:16.030 --> 01:43:17.999
for one or two issues on the provider side
to get sorted and they are now done, and the

01:43:17.999 --> 01:43:18.999
annual reports are then drafted, and I think
we are close to being ready to sign off, he

01:43:18.999 --> 01:43:19.999
says.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: When do you think they will be

01:43:19.999 --> 01:43:20.999
laid before parliament?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So that is - can I come back

01:43:20.999 --> 01:43:21.999
and at least tell the board outside because
I honestly - I don't have the date in my head.

01:43:21.999 --> 01:43:22.999
We're trying to get them done before - I mean,
they actually need to have been laid before

01:43:22.999 --> 01:43:23.999
summer recess, so they pretty much need to
be laid within the next two weeks, and I think

01:43:23.999 --> 01:43:24.999
we are able to do it, indeed within the next
week or so. So my apologies for not being

01:43:24.999 --> 01:43:25.999
&gt;&gt;Speaker: No, that was the key point. I mean,
from our point of view, we would like these

01:43:25.999 --> 01:43:26.999
transparent reviews to be published as soon
as possible, i.e. get them laid before the

01:43:26.999 --> 01:43:30.010
summer break, sometimes there have been delays,
both from other government business, but as

01:43:30.010 --> 01:43:32.309
a busy outside of our control. That's two
organisations.

01:43:32.309 --> 01:43:33.512
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I think that we sometimes desire
to have all [inaudible] on the day, the department

01:43:33.512 --> 01:43:34.949
itself, which has been I think the issue in
the past about the timing.

01:43:34.949 --> 01:43:35.949
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Well, we're beautifully [inaudible]
he and I, anyway, so that's not going to be

01:43:35.949 --> 01:43:36.949
the rate-limiting factor this year.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes. Indeed. Good. Andrew,

01:43:36.949 --> 01:43:46.409
is there anything for the people committee?
Ara's not here, and we've had a long DQPC

01:43:46.409 --> 01:43:53.030
this morning. I think we should mark - I think
this is only our second cycle of all our sub-committees,

01:43:53.030 --> 01:43:59.280
and you know, I'd like to say thank you to
Jessica and the board secretariat team. It

01:43:59.280 --> 01:44:04.280
has been no mean feat trying to coordinate
us all and create that structure, and there's

01:44:04.280 --> 01:44:09.420
clearly lots more for us to keep learning
and improving, so it's a major step forward,

01:44:09.420 --> 01:44:14.830
so thank you. And you get to have the final
item, a governments update. Could you talk

01:44:14.830 --> 01:44:16.949
to us?
&gt;&gt;Jessica Dahlstrom: Yes. This paper provides

01:44:16.949 --> 01:44:23.729
the board with an update on the progress made
on developing a shared government model for

01:44:23.729 --> 01:44:30.501
both organisations. We, as Dido mentioned,
have committees in common in place now. We

01:44:30.501 --> 01:44:36.540
are currently working through the schemes
of delegation for both NHS England and NHS

01:44:36.540 --> 01:44:42.920
Improvement to make some further amendments
as the operating model changes, but for today,

01:44:42.920 --> 01:44:47.769
what we would like to present to the board
as the final terms of reference for the provide

01:44:47.769 --> 01:44:54.920
oversight committee and the regional support
groups which are both executive committees,

01:44:54.920 --> 01:45:00.340
but in the case of the provider oversight
committee reporting into the NHS Improvement

01:45:00.340 --> 01:45:06.110
board, that deal with both the provider and
in the regional support group on the commissioning

01:45:06.110 --> 01:45:10.380
side of our executive business.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Ian, do you want to just say

01:45:10.380 --> 01:45:14.570
anything about the provider oversight board
as the chair? The provide oversight committee,

01:45:14.570 --> 01:45:18.909
sorry?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dalton: Yes. I mean, I think it is undoubtedly

01:45:18.909 --> 01:45:26.690
the case that, notwithstanding the fact that
our job is to support improvement rather than

01:45:26.690 --> 01:45:32.360
some regulations in our predominant [inaudible],
nonetheless there are some stuffing duties

01:45:32.360 --> 01:45:38.449
and decisions that are reserved to NHS Improvement,
in relation to provider regulation, the oversight

01:45:38.449 --> 01:45:45.690
of transactions and investments, and the provide
oversight committee provides an appropriate

01:45:45.690 --> 01:45:51.809
governance shelf for those decisions, including
when organisations are formally entered into

01:45:51.809 --> 01:45:57.719
and exit from special measures, when provide
a regulatory action is necessary to be taken.

01:45:57.719 --> 01:46:03.139
So, I think it's important clarification that
as we come together, very, very appropriately

01:46:03.139 --> 01:46:08.599
as NHS England and NHS Improvement working
together, nonetheless there needs to be an

01:46:08.599 --> 01:46:15.559
appropriate secure governance forum in which
provider regulatory decisions can be taken.

01:46:15.559 --> 01:46:21.080
And that is exactly what this committee is
now established and already working to undertake.

01:46:21.080 --> 01:46:26.400
So, I think it's a necessary and important
piece of our collective governance, and one

01:46:26.400 --> 01:46:30.790
that I am glad is in place.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any questions or comments?

01:46:30.790 --> 01:46:40.090
We are happy to note it. Excellent. In which
case, I think we can draw to a close the public

01:46:40.090 --> 01:46:45.000
meeting, and we should finish by another thank
you to Wendy, to Matthew, and to Ian, except

01:46:45.000 --> 01:46:49.979
to say that, actually, your work isn't yet
done as we have to reconvene in private session

01:46:49.979 --> 01:46:55.809
in a few minutes. But I think we should at
least and the public meeting by recording

01:46:55.809 --> 01:46:56.519
our thanks again. Thanks very much, everyone.

